window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2016-06-2", "source": "2016年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay on the use of robots.Try to imagine what will happen when more and more robots take the place of human beings in industry as well as people's daily lives. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Robots are no longer the stuff of science fiction; increasingly, they are moving into our factories and our\nhomes. In the foreseeable future, when more and more machines take over tasks once done by people,\nboth industrial production and everyday life will be reshaped. To my mind, this shift will prove a double-\nedged sword.\nOn the bright side, the changes will be striking. On the assembly line, tireless robots will weld, sort and\npackage around the clock, lifting efficiency and freeing workers from dull, dangerous jobs. At home, they will\nvacuum the floors, cook meals and even care for the elderly, giving families more time to relax. Yet every coin\nhas two sides. As machines replace human hands, millions may lose their jobs, and an over-reliance on\ntechnology could weaken our own skills, raising thorny ethical questions.\nTherefore, I predict that robots will become our capable partners rather than our masters, provided we learn to\nadapt. If society retrains workers and sets clear rules, this quiet revolution will make life not colder, but far\nmore convenient."}, "translation": {"prompt": "旗袍(qipao) 是一种雅致的中国服装，源于中国的满族(Manchu Nationality)。在清代，旗袍是王室女性穿着的宽松长袍上世纪20年代，受西方服饰影响，旗袍发生了一些变化。袖口(cuffs) 变窄，袍身变短。这些变化使女性美得以充分展现。 如今，旗袍经常出现在世界级的时装秀上。中国女性出席重要社交聚会时，旗袍往往是她们的首选。很多中国新娘也会选择旗袍作为结婚礼服。一些有影响的人士甚至建议将旗袍作为中国女性的民族服饰。", "sample": "Qipao is an elegant Chinese garment originating from the Manchu people. During the Qing Dynasty, it was a loose robe worn by royal women. In the 1920s, influenced by Western clothing, the qipao underwent some changes: the cuffs became narrower and the robe became shorter. These changes fully displayed the beauty of women. Today, the qipao often appears at world-class fashion shows. When Chinese women attend important social gatherings, the qipao is often their first choice. Many Chinese brides also choose the qipao as their wedding dress. Some influential people even suggest making the qipao the national costume for Chinese women."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2016-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Let's say you love roller-skating. Just the thought of  __26__  on your roller-skates brings a smile to your face. You also know that roller-skating is excellent exercise. You have a  __27__  attitude toward it.\nThis description of roller-skating  __28__  the three components of an attitude: affect, cognition, and behavior. You love the activity; it's great fun. These feelings  __29__  the affective or emotional component; they are an important ingredient in attitudes. The knowledge we have about the object constitutes the cognitive component of an attitude. You understand the health  __30__  that the activity can bring. Finally, attitudes have a behavioral component. Our attitudes  __31__  us to go outside to enjoy roller-skating.\nNow, we don't want to leave you with the  __32__  that these three components always work together  __33__  . They don't; sometimes they clash. For example, let's say you love pizza (affective component); however, you have high cholesterol and understand (knowledge component) that eating pizza may be bad for your health. Which behavior will your attitude result in, eating pizza or  __34__  it? The answer depends on which component happens to be stronger. If you are walking past a pizza restaurant at lunchtime, your emotions and feelings probably will be stronger than your knowledge that pizza may not be the best food for your health. In that instance, you have pizza for lunch. If you are at home trying to decide where to go for dinner, however, the knowledge component may  __35__  , and you decide to go where you can eat a healthier meal.", "matchIntro": "The Changing Generation", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "It turns out today's teenagers aren't so scary after all. Results of USA WEEKEND's Teens & Parents survey reveal a generation of young people who get along well with their parents and approve of the way they're being raised. They think of their parents with affection and respect. They speak with Mom or Dad when they have a problem. Most feel that their parents understand them, and they believe their family is the No. 1 priority in their parents' lives. Many even think their parents are cool! Although more than a third have an object in their rooms they would like to keep secret from their parents, rarely is it anything more alarming than a diary or off-color(低俗的) book or CD."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Such results may seem surprising against the background of shocking incidents that color the way the mass media portray the young. In October 2000, the same month the survey was taken, the Washington-based Center for Media and Public Affairs wrote in its publication Media Monitor that, in a recent month of TV news coverage of American youth, just 2% of teens were shown at home, and just 1% were portrayed in a work setting. In contrast, the criminal justice system accounted for nearly one out of every five visual backgrounds. No wonder parents worry their own kids might spin out of control once they hit the turbulent waters of adolescence."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The overall facts ought to reassure us. The survey shows us that today's teens are affectionate, sensible and far happier than the angry and tortured souls that have been painted for us by stereotypes. From other sources, we also know teenage crime, drug abuse and premarital sex are in general decline. We, of course, need to pay attention to youngsters who are filled with discontent and hostility, but we should not allow these extreme cases to distort our view of most young people."}, {"label": "D", "text": "My own research at the Stanford Center on Adolescence uses in-depth interviews with small samples of youngsters rather than large-scale surveys. Still, in my studies and others I have read, I find the same patterns as in USA WEEKEND's survey. Today's teenagers admire their parents and welcome parental guidance about important matters such as career choice—though certainly not Mom and Dad's advice on matters of personal taste, such as music or fashion. When we ask teens to choose a hero, they usually select an older family member rather than a remote public figure. Most teens say they enjoy the company of both parents and friends."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Contrary to some stereotypes, most adolescents believe they must be tolerant of differences among individuals (though they do not always find this easy in the cliquish(拉帮结派的) environment of high school). Many of them volunteer for community service with disadvantaged people. One prevalent quality we have found in teens' statements about themselves, their friends and their families is a strikingly positive emotional tone. By and large, these are very nice kids, and as the band The Who used to sing, \"The kids are alright.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "How much is today's spirit of harmony a change from our more turbulent past? A mere generation ago, parent-child relations were described as \"the generation gap\". Yet even then reports of widespread youth rebellion were overdone: Most kids in the '60s and '70s shared their parents' basic values. Still, it is true that American families are growing closer at the dawn of this new millennium(千年). Perhaps there is less to fight about, with the country in a period of tranquility and the dangers of drug abuse and other unwholesome behavior well known. Perhaps in the face of impersonal and intimidating globalization, a young person's family feels more like a friendly haven than an oppressive trap. And perhaps parents are acting more like parents than in the recent past. Within just the past five years, I have noticed parents returning to a belief that teenagers need the guidance of elders rather than the liberal, \"anything goes\" mode of child-rearing that became popular in the second half of the 20th century."}, {"label": "G", "text": "But missing from all these data is the sense that today's young care very much about their country, about the broader civic and political environment, or about the future of their society. They seem to be turning inward-generally in a pro-social manner, certainly with positive benefits for intimate relationships, but too often at the expense of a connection with the present and future world beyond, including the society they will one day inherit."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Recently, we examined more than 400 essays on the \"laws of life\" that teens from two communities had written as part of an educational program initiated by the John Templeton Foundation in Radnor, Pa. In those essays, and in follow-up interviews with a few of the teenagers, we found lots of insight, positive feeling and inspirational thinking. But we also found little interest in civic life beyond the tight circles of their family and immediate friends."}, {"label": "I", "text": "For example, only one boy said he would like to be president when he grows up. When I was in high school, dozens in my class alone would have answered differently. In fact, other recent studies have found there has never been a time in American history when so small a proportion of young people have sought or accepted leadership roles in local civic organizations. It is also troubling that voting rates among our youngest eligible voters—18- to 24-year-olds—are way down: Little more than one in four now go to the polls, even in national elections, compared with almost twice that many when 18-year-olds were first given the vote."}, {"label": "J", "text": "In our interviews, many students viewed politics with suspicion and distaste. \"Most politicians are kind of crooked(不诚实的),\" one student declared. Another, discussing national politics, said, \"I feel like one person can't do that much, and I get the impression most people don't think a group of people can do that much.\" Asked what they would like to change in the world, the students mentioned only personal concerns such as slowing down the pace of life, gaining good friends, becoming more spiritual, becoming either more materially successful or less materially oriented (depending on the student's values), and being more respectful of the Earth, animals and other people. One boy said, \"I'd rather be concentrating on artistic efforts than saving the world or something.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "It is fine and healthy for teens to cultivate their personal interests, and it is good news when young people enjoy harmonious relations with their family and friends. But there is also a place in a young life for noble purposes that include a dedication to the broader society, a love of country and an aspiration to make their own leadership contributions."}, {"label": "L", "text": "In the past, the young have eagerly participated in national service and civic affairs, often with lots of energy and idealism. If this is not happening today, we should ask why. Our society needs the full participation of its younger citizens if it is to continue to thrive. We know the promise is there—this is a well-grounded, talented, warm-hearted group of youngsters. We have everything to gain by encouraging them to explore the world beyond their immediate experience and to prepare themselves for their turn at shaping that world."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Manufacturers of products that claim to be environmentally friendly will face tighter rules on how they are advertised to consumers under changes proposed by the Federal Trade Commission.\nThe commission's revised \"Green Guides\" warn marketers against using labels that make broad claims, like \"eco-friendly\". Marketers must qualify their claims on the product packaging and limit them to a specific benefit, such as how much of the product is recycled.\n\"This is really about trying to cut through the confusion that consumers have when they are buying a product and that businesses have when they are selling a product,\" said Jon Leibowitz, chairman of the commission.\nThe revisions come at a time when green marketing is on the rise. According to a new study, the number of advertisements with green messages in mainstream magazines has risen since 1987, and peaked in 2008 at 10.4%. In 2009, the number dropped to 9%.\nBut while the number of advertisements may have dipped, there has been a rapid spread of eco-labeling. There are both good and bad players in the eco-labeling game.\nIn the last five years or so, there has been an explosion of green claims and environmental claims. It is clear that consumers don't always know what they are getting.\nA handful of lawsuits have been filed in recent years against companies accused of using misleading environmental labels. In 2008 and 2009, class-action lawsuits(集体诉讼) were filed against SC Johnson for using \"Greenlist\" labels on its cleaning products. The lawsuits said that the label was misleading because it gave the impression that the products had been certified by a third party when the certification was the company's own.\n\"We are very proud of our accomplishments under the Greenlist system and we believe that we will prevail in these cases,\" Christopher Beard, director of public affairs for SC Johnson, said, while acknowledging that \"this has been an area that is difficult to navigate.\"\nCompanies have also taken it upon themselves to contest each other's green claims.\nDavid Mallen, associate director of the Council of Better Business Bureau, said in the last two years the organization had seen an increase in the number of claims companies were bringing against each other for false or misleading environmental product claims.\n\"About once a week, I have a client that will bring up a new certification I've never even heard of and I'm in this industry,\" said Kevin Wilhelm, chief executive officer of Sustainable Business Consulting. \"It's kind of a Wild West, anybody can claim themselves to be green.\" Mr. Wilhelm said the excess of labels made it difficult for businesses and consumers to know which labels they should pay attention to.", "47": "Manufacturers of products that claim to be environmentally friendly will face tighter rules on how they are advertised to consumers under changes proposed by the Federal Trade Commission.\nThe commission's revised \"Green Guides\" warn marketers against using labels that make broad claims, like \"eco-friendly\". Marketers must qualify their claims on the product packaging and limit them to a specific benefit, such as how much of the product is recycled.\n\"This is really about trying to cut through the confusion that consumers have when they are buying a product and that businesses have when they are selling a product,\" said Jon Leibowitz, chairman of the commission.\nThe revisions come at a time when green marketing is on the rise. According to a new study, the number of advertisements with green messages in mainstream magazines has risen since 1987, and peaked in 2008 at 10.4%. In 2009, the number dropped to 9%.\nBut while the number of advertisements may have dipped, there has been a rapid spread of eco-labeling. There are both good and bad players in the eco-labeling game.\nIn the last five years or so, there has been an explosion of green claims and environmental claims. It is clear that consumers don't always know what they are getting.\nA handful of lawsuits have been filed in recent years against companies accused of using misleading environmental labels. In 2008 and 2009, class-action lawsuits(集体诉讼) were filed against SC Johnson for using \"Greenlist\" labels on its cleaning products. The lawsuits said that the label was misleading because it gave the impression that the products had been certified by a third party when the certification was the company's own.\n\"We are very proud of our accomplishments under the Greenlist system and we believe that we will prevail in these cases,\" Christopher Beard, director of public affairs for SC Johnson, said, while acknowledging that \"this has been an area that is difficult to navigate.\"\nCompanies have also taken it upon themselves to contest each other's green claims.\nDavid Mallen, associate director of the Council of Better Business Bureau, said in the last two years the organization had seen an increase in the number of claims companies were bringing against each other for false or misleading environmental product claims.\n\"About once a week, I have a client that will bring up a new certification I've never even heard of and I'm in this industry,\" said Kevin Wilhelm, chief executive officer of Sustainable Business Consulting. \"It's kind of a Wild West, anybody can claim themselves to be green.\" Mr. Wilhelm said the excess of labels made it difficult for businesses and consumers to know which labels they should pay attention to.", "48": "Manufacturers of products that claim to be environmentally friendly will face tighter rules on how they are advertised to consumers under changes proposed by the Federal Trade Commission.\nThe commission's revised \"Green Guides\" warn marketers against using labels that make broad claims, like \"eco-friendly\". Marketers must qualify their claims on the product packaging and limit them to a specific benefit, such as how much of the product is recycled.\n\"This is really about trying to cut through the confusion that consumers have when they are buying a product and that businesses have when they are selling a product,\" said Jon Leibowitz, chairman of the commission.\nThe revisions come at a time when green marketing is on the rise. According to a new study, the number of advertisements with green messages in mainstream magazines has risen since 1987, and peaked in 2008 at 10.4%. In 2009, the number dropped to 9%.\nBut while the number of advertisements may have dipped, there has been a rapid spread of eco-labeling. There are both good and bad players in the eco-labeling game.\nIn the last five years or so, there has been an explosion of green claims and environmental claims. It is clear that consumers don't always know what they are getting.\nA handful of lawsuits have been filed in recent years against companies accused of using misleading environmental labels. In 2008 and 2009, class-action lawsuits(集体诉讼) were filed against SC Johnson for using \"Greenlist\" labels on its cleaning products. The lawsuits said that the label was misleading because it gave the impression that the products had been certified by a third party when the certification was the company's own.\n\"We are very proud of our accomplishments under the Greenlist system and we believe that we will prevail in these cases,\" Christopher Beard, director of public affairs for SC Johnson, said, while acknowledging that \"this has been an area that is difficult to navigate.\"\nCompanies have also taken it upon themselves to contest each other's green claims.\nDavid Mallen, associate director of the Council of Better Business Bureau, said in the last two years the organization had seen an increase in the number of claims companies were bringing against each other for false or misleading environmental product claims.\n\"About once a week, I have a client that will bring up a new certification I've never even heard of and I'm in this industry,\" said Kevin Wilhelm, chief executive officer of Sustainable Business Consulting. \"It's kind of a Wild West, anybody can claim themselves to be green.\" Mr. Wilhelm said the excess of labels made it difficult for businesses and consumers to know which labels they should pay attention to.", "49": "Manufacturers of products that claim to be environmentally friendly will face tighter rules on how they are advertised to consumers under changes proposed by the Federal Trade Commission.\nThe commission's revised \"Green Guides\" warn marketers against using labels that make broad claims, like \"eco-friendly\". Marketers must qualify their claims on the product packaging and limit them to a specific benefit, such as how much of the product is recycled.\n\"This is really about trying to cut through the confusion that consumers have when they are buying a product and that businesses have when they are selling a product,\" said Jon Leibowitz, chairman of the commission.\nThe revisions come at a time when green marketing is on the rise. According to a new study, the number of advertisements with green messages in mainstream magazines has risen since 1987, and peaked in 2008 at 10.4%. In 2009, the number dropped to 9%.\nBut while the number of advertisements may have dipped, there has been a rapid spread of eco-labeling. There are both good and bad players in the eco-labeling game.\nIn the last five years or so, there has been an explosion of green claims and environmental claims. It is clear that consumers don't always know what they are getting.\nA handful of lawsuits have been filed in recent years against companies accused of using misleading environmental labels. In 2008 and 2009, class-action lawsuits(集体诉讼) were filed against SC Johnson for using \"Greenlist\" labels on its cleaning products. The lawsuits said that the label was misleading because it gave the impression that the products had been certified by a third party when the certification was the company's own.\n\"We are very proud of our accomplishments under the Greenlist system and we believe that we will prevail in these cases,\" Christopher Beard, director of public affairs for SC Johnson, said, while acknowledging that \"this has been an area that is difficult to navigate.\"\nCompanies have also taken it upon themselves to contest each other's green claims.\nDavid Mallen, associate director of the Council of Better Business Bureau, said in the last two years the organization had seen an increase in the number of claims companies were bringing against each other for false or misleading environmental product claims.\n\"About once a week, I have a client that will bring up a new certification I've never even heard of and I'm in this industry,\" said Kevin Wilhelm, chief executive officer of Sustainable Business Consulting. \"It's kind of a Wild West, anybody can claim themselves to be green.\" Mr. Wilhelm said the excess of labels made it difficult for businesses and consumers to know which labels they should pay attention to.", "50": "Manufacturers of products that claim to be environmentally friendly will face tighter rules on how they are advertised to consumers under changes proposed by the Federal Trade Commission.\nThe commission's revised \"Green Guides\" warn marketers against using labels that make broad claims, like \"eco-friendly\". Marketers must qualify their claims on the product packaging and limit them to a specific benefit, such as how much of the product is recycled.\n\"This is really about trying to cut through the confusion that consumers have when they are buying a product and that businesses have when they are selling a product,\" said Jon Leibowitz, chairman of the commission.\nThe revisions come at a time when green marketing is on the rise. According to a new study, the number of advertisements with green messages in mainstream magazines has risen since 1987, and peaked in 2008 at 10.4%. In 2009, the number dropped to 9%.\nBut while the number of advertisements may have dipped, there has been a rapid spread of eco-labeling. There are both good and bad players in the eco-labeling game.\nIn the last five years or so, there has been an explosion of green claims and environmental claims. It is clear that consumers don't always know what they are getting.\nA handful of lawsuits have been filed in recent years against companies accused of using misleading environmental labels. In 2008 and 2009, class-action lawsuits(集体诉讼) were filed against SC Johnson for using \"Greenlist\" labels on its cleaning products. The lawsuits said that the label was misleading because it gave the impression that the products had been certified by a third party when the certification was the company's own.\n\"We are very proud of our accomplishments under the Greenlist system and we believe that we will prevail in these cases,\" Christopher Beard, director of public affairs for SC Johnson, said, while acknowledging that \"this has been an area that is difficult to navigate.\"\nCompanies have also taken it upon themselves to contest each other's green claims.\nDavid Mallen, associate director of the Council of Better Business Bureau, said in the last two years the organization had seen an increase in the number of claims companies were bringing against each other for false or misleading environmental product claims.\n\"About once a week, I have a client that will bring up a new certification I've never even heard of and I'm in this industry,\" said Kevin Wilhelm, chief executive officer of Sustainable Business Consulting. \"It's kind of a Wild West, anybody can claim themselves to be green.\" Mr. Wilhelm said the excess of labels made it difficult for businesses and consumers to know which labels they should pay attention to.", "51": "America's education system has become less a ladder of opportunity than a structure to transmit inequality from one generation to the next.\nThat's why school reform is so critical. This is an issue of equality, opportunity and national conscience. It's not just about education, but about poverty and justice.\nIt's true that the main reason inner-city schools do poorly isn't teachers' unions, but poverty. Southern states without strong teachers' unions have schools at least as awful as those in union states. Some Chicago teachers seem to think that they shouldn't be held accountable until poverty is solved. There're steps we can take that would make some difference, and Mayor Rahm Emanuel is trying some of them—yet the union is resisting.\nI'd be sympathetic if the union focused solely on higher compensation. Teachers need to be much better paid to attract the best college graduates to the nation's worst schools. But, instead, the Chicago union seems to be using its political capital primarily to protect weak performers.\nThere's solid evidence that there are huge differences in the effectiveness of teachers. The gold standard study by Harvard and Columbia University scholars found that even in high-poverty schools, teachers consistently had a huge positive or negative impact.\nGet a bottom 1% teacher, and the effect is the same as if a child misses 40% of the school year. Get a teacher from the top 20%, and it's as if a child has gone to school for an extra month or two. The study found that strong teachers in the fourth through eighth grades raised the skills of their students in ways that would last for decades. Just having a strong teacher for one elementary year left pupils a bit less likely to become mothers as teenagers, a bit more likely to go to college and earning more money at age 28.\nHow does one figure out who is a weak teacher? Yes, that's a challenge. But researchers are improving systems to measure a teacher's performance throughout the year, and, with three years of data, it's usually possible to tell which teachers are failing.\nUnfortunately, the union in Chicago is insisting that teachers who are laid off—often for being ineffective—should get priority in new hiring. That's an insult to students.\nTeaching is so important that it should be like other professions, with high pay and good working conditions but few job protections for bottom performers.\nThis isn't a battle between garment workers and greedy bosses. The central figures in the Chicago schools strike are neither strikers nor managers but 350,000 children. Protecting the union demand sacrifices those students, in effect turning a blind eye to the injustice in the education system.", "52": "America's education system has become less a ladder of opportunity than a structure to transmit inequality from one generation to the next.\nThat's why school reform is so critical. This is an issue of equality, opportunity and national conscience. It's not just about education, but about poverty and justice.\nIt's true that the main reason inner-city schools do poorly isn't teachers' unions, but poverty. Southern states without strong teachers' unions have schools at least as awful as those in union states. Some Chicago teachers seem to think that they shouldn't be held accountable until poverty is solved. There're steps we can take that would make some difference, and Mayor Rahm Emanuel is trying some of them—yet the union is resisting.\nI'd be sympathetic if the union focused solely on higher compensation. Teachers need to be much better paid to attract the best college graduates to the nation's worst schools. But, instead, the Chicago union seems to be using its political capital primarily to protect weak performers.\nThere's solid evidence that there are huge differences in the effectiveness of teachers. The gold standard study by Harvard and Columbia University scholars found that even in high-poverty schools, teachers consistently had a huge positive or negative impact.\nGet a bottom 1% teacher, and the effect is the same as if a child misses 40% of the school year. Get a teacher from the top 20%, and it's as if a child has gone to school for an extra month or two. The study found that strong teachers in the fourth through eighth grades raised the skills of their students in ways that would last for decades. Just having a strong teacher for one elementary year left pupils a bit less likely to become mothers as teenagers, a bit more likely to go to college and earning more money at age 28.\nHow does one figure out who is a weak teacher? Yes, that's a challenge. But researchers are improving systems to measure a teacher's performance throughout the year, and, with three years of data, it's usually possible to tell which teachers are failing.\nUnfortunately, the union in Chicago is insisting that teachers who are laid off—often for being ineffective—should get priority in new hiring. That's an insult to students.\nTeaching is so important that it should be like other professions, with high pay and good working conditions but few job protections for bottom performers.\nThis isn't a battle between garment workers and greedy bosses. The central figures in the Chicago schools strike are neither strikers nor managers but 350,000 children. Protecting the union demand sacrifices those students, in effect turning a blind eye to the injustice in the education system.", "53": "America's education system has become less a ladder of opportunity than a structure to transmit inequality from one generation to the next.\nThat's why school reform is so critical. This is an issue of equality, opportunity and national conscience. It's not just about education, but about poverty and justice.\nIt's true that the main reason inner-city schools do poorly isn't teachers' unions, but poverty. Southern states without strong teachers' unions have schools at least as awful as those in union states. Some Chicago teachers seem to think that they shouldn't be held accountable until poverty is solved. There're steps we can take that would make some difference, and Mayor Rahm Emanuel is trying some of them—yet the union is resisting.\nI'd be sympathetic if the union focused solely on higher compensation. Teachers need to be much better paid to attract the best college graduates to the nation's worst schools. But, instead, the Chicago union seems to be using its political capital primarily to protect weak performers.\nThere's solid evidence that there are huge differences in the effectiveness of teachers. The gold standard study by Harvard and Columbia University scholars found that even in high-poverty schools, teachers consistently had a huge positive or negative impact.\nGet a bottom 1% teacher, and the effect is the same as if a child misses 40% of the school year. Get a teacher from the top 20%, and it's as if a child has gone to school for an extra month or two. The study found that strong teachers in the fourth through eighth grades raised the skills of their students in ways that would last for decades. Just having a strong teacher for one elementary year left pupils a bit less likely to become mothers as teenagers, a bit more likely to go to college and earning more money at age 28.\nHow does one figure out who is a weak teacher? Yes, that's a challenge. But researchers are improving systems to measure a teacher's performance throughout the year, and, with three years of data, it's usually possible to tell which teachers are failing.\nUnfortunately, the union in Chicago is insisting that teachers who are laid off—often for being ineffective—should get priority in new hiring. That's an insult to students.\nTeaching is so important that it should be like other professions, with high pay and good working conditions but few job protections for bottom performers.\nThis isn't a battle between garment workers and greedy bosses. The central figures in the Chicago schools strike are neither strikers nor managers but 350,000 children. Protecting the union demand sacrifices those students, in effect turning a blind eye to the injustice in the education system.", "54": "America's education system has become less a ladder of opportunity than a structure to transmit inequality from one generation to the next.\nThat's why school reform is so critical. This is an issue of equality, opportunity and national conscience. It's not just about education, but about poverty and justice.\nIt's true that the main reason inner-city schools do poorly isn't teachers' unions, but poverty. Southern states without strong teachers' unions have schools at least as awful as those in union states. Some Chicago teachers seem to think that they shouldn't be held accountable until poverty is solved. There're steps we can take that would make some difference, and Mayor Rahm Emanuel is trying some of them—yet the union is resisting.\nI'd be sympathetic if the union focused solely on higher compensation. Teachers need to be much better paid to attract the best college graduates to the nation's worst schools. But, instead, the Chicago union seems to be using its political capital primarily to protect weak performers.\nThere's solid evidence that there are huge differences in the effectiveness of teachers. The gold standard study by Harvard and Columbia University scholars found that even in high-poverty schools, teachers consistently had a huge positive or negative impact.\nGet a bottom 1% teacher, and the effect is the same as if a child misses 40% of the school year. Get a teacher from the top 20%, and it's as if a child has gone to school for an extra month or two. The study found that strong teachers in the fourth through eighth grades raised the skills of their students in ways that would last for decades. Just having a strong teacher for one elementary year left pupils a bit less likely to become mothers as teenagers, a bit more likely to go to college and earning more money at age 28.\nHow does one figure out who is a weak teacher? Yes, that's a challenge. But researchers are improving systems to measure a teacher's performance throughout the year, and, with three years of data, it's usually possible to tell which teachers are failing.\nUnfortunately, the union in Chicago is insisting that teachers who are laid off—often for being ineffective—should get priority in new hiring. That's an insult to students.\nTeaching is so important that it should be like other professions, with high pay and good working conditions but few job protections for bottom performers.\nThis isn't a battle between garment workers and greedy bosses. The central figures in the Chicago schools strike are neither strikers nor managers but 350,000 children. Protecting the union demand sacrifices those students, in effect turning a blind eye to the injustice in the education system.", "55": "America's education system has become less a ladder of opportunity than a structure to transmit inequality from one generation to the next.\nThat's why school reform is so critical. This is an issue of equality, opportunity and national conscience. It's not just about education, but about poverty and justice.\nIt's true that the main reason inner-city schools do poorly isn't teachers' unions, but poverty. Southern states without strong teachers' unions have schools at least as awful as those in union states. Some Chicago teachers seem to think that they shouldn't be held accountable until poverty is solved. There're steps we can take that would make some difference, and Mayor Rahm Emanuel is trying some of them—yet the union is resisting.\nI'd be sympathetic if the union focused solely on higher compensation. Teachers need to be much better paid to attract the best college graduates to the nation's worst schools. But, instead, the Chicago union seems to be using its political capital primarily to protect weak performers.\nThere's solid evidence that there are huge differences in the effectiveness of teachers. The gold standard study by Harvard and Columbia University scholars found that even in high-poverty schools, teachers consistently had a huge positive or negative impact.\nGet a bottom 1% teacher, and the effect is the same as if a child misses 40% of the school year. Get a teacher from the top 20%, and it's as if a child has gone to school for an extra month or two. The study found that strong teachers in the fourth through eighth grades raised the skills of their students in ways that would last for decades. Just having a strong teacher for one elementary year left pupils a bit less likely to become mothers as teenagers, a bit more likely to go to college and earning more money at age 28.\nHow does one figure out who is a weak teacher? Yes, that's a challenge. But researchers are improving systems to measure a teacher's performance throughout the year, and, with three years of data, it's usually possible to tell which teachers are failing.\nUnfortunately, the union in Chicago is insisting that teachers who are laid off—often for being ineffective—should get priority in new hiring. That's an insult to students.\nTeaching is so important that it should be like other professions, with high pay and good working conditions but few job protections for bottom performers.\nThis isn't a battle between garment workers and greedy bosses. The central figures in the Chicago schools strike are neither strikers nor managers but 350,000 children. Protecting the union demand sacrifices those students, in effect turning a blind eye to the injustice in the education system."}, "questions": [{"id": "2016-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What seems to have been very successful according to the woman speaker?", "options": ["A) The project the man managed at Cucin Tech.", "B) The updating of technology at Cucin Tech.", "C) The man's switch to a new career.", "D) The restructuring of her company."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The project the man managed at Cucin Tech.\n【定位】W: So, Mike, you managed the innovation project at Cucin Tech. W: It seems to have been\nvery successful. M: Yes. I really helped things turn around at Cucin Tech.\n女：Mike，你负责了 Cucin Tech 的创新项目。女：它看来非常成功。男：是的，我确实帮\nCucin Tech 扭转了局面。\n【信号】题干原话回指首句话题。女方先点出话题 you managed the innovation project at Cucin\nTech，随后说 It seems to have been very successful，题干的 successful 正是直接回指\n这个\"innovation project\"，定位句就在开头。\n【替换】the innovation project at Cucin Tech ↔ The project the man managed at Cucin Tech\n近乎原句对应\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThe updating of technology at Cucin Tech 借了公司名 Cucin Tech 和 technology 一词\n诱你\"听到就选\"，但录音说成功的是 innovation project，不是\"技术更新\"，且后文 new\nproducts being created simply because the technology is there 反而是被否定的做法。\n借⽤原⽂：the innovation project at Cucin Tech\nC) 未提及\nThe man's switch to a new career 在录音里找不到对应，男方只谈他在 Cucin Tech 做的\n项目，从未提换职业；这是凭\"the man\"这个共同主语硬凑的干扰。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe restructuring of her company 把对象换成女方的公司(her)，并加了录音没提的\nrestructuring(重组)；成功的是男方负责的项目，主语张冠李戴。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the company lack before the man's scheme was implemented?", "options": ["A) Talented personnel.", "B) Strategic innovation.", "C) Competitive products.", "D) Effective promotion."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Strategic innovation.\n【定位】W: Was the revival in their fortunes entirely due to strategic innovation? M: Yes, yes. I think\nit was. Cucin Tech was a company who were very much following the pack, doing what\neveryone else was doing, and getting rapidly left behind.\n女：他们时来运转完全是靠策略性创新吗？男：是的，是的，我认为是。Cucin Tech 当初\n是一家一味随大流、跟着别人做、迅速被甩在后头的公司。\n【信号】被问者对\"是不是靠创新\"的应答。女方问 Was the revival...entirely due to strategic\ninnovation?，男方答 Yes, yes. I think it was，确认\"扭转靠的正是策略性创新\"——反推改\n变前公司缺的就是 strategic innovation；信号是被问者的肯定应答。\n【替换】strategic innovation ↔ Strategic innovation\n确认即原词对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTalented personnel 利用录音 I could see there was a lot of talent there 里的 talent 诱\n选，但男方说公司\"本来就有不少人才\"，恰恰是\"有\"而非\"缺\"，极性正好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：there was a lot of talent there\nC) 原词陷阱\nCompetitive products 借了 particularly in their product development 里的 product 诱\n选，但录音说的是公司在产品开发上\"有潜力\"，并非缺有竞争力的产品。\n借⽤原⽂：some great potential, particularly in their product development\nD) 未提及\nEffective promotion 在录音里没有任何对应，男方通篇谈创新与人才潜力，未提\"推广/营\n销\"，属凭常识硬补。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the man say he should do in his business?", "options": ["A) Expand the market.", "B) Recruit more talents.", "C) Innovate constantly.", "D) Watch out for his competitors."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Innovate constantly.\n【定位】M: We need to be innovating constantly to keep up with this. Stand still, and you're lost.\n男：我们需要不断创新以跟上变化。原地不动，你就被淘汰了。\n【信号】we need to 表主张。We need to be innovating constantly to keep up with this 用 we\nneed to 直接表态\"必须不断创新\"，再加 Stand still, and you're lost 强化，这就是男方主\n张该做的事。\n【替换】We need to be innovating constantly ↔ Innovate constantly\n动词形式微调\n【排除】A) 未提及\nExpand the market 录音从未提及，男方谈的是创新而非市场扩张，属凭商业常识补的干\n扰。\nB) 原词陷阱\nRecruit more talents 借了前文 a lot of talent there 的 talent 一词诱选，但男方谈的\n是\"用好现有人才(harness that)\"，并未主张\"再招更多人\"。\n借⽤原⽂：I could see there was a lot of talent there\nD) 原词陷阱\nWatch out for his competitors 借了后文 difficult for a competitor to copy 的\ncompetitor 诱选，但男方主张的是\"不断创新\"，没说\"提防对手\"；competitor 出现在讲风\n险的另一处。\n借⽤原⽂：difficult for a competitor to copy", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man say is the risk of innovation?", "options": ["A) Possible bankruptcy.", "B) Unforeseen difficulties.", "C) Conflicts within the company.", "D) Imitation by one's competitors."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Imitation by one's competitors.\n【定位】M: The risk is of course that innovation may frequently lead to imitation.\n男：当然，风险在于创新常常会招来模仿。\n【信号】The risk is 直接点风险。The risk is of course that innovation may frequently lead to\nimitation 用 The risk is 明确点出风险，紧跟其后的 imitation 就是答案，信号词就是 risk\n本身。\n【替换】innovation may frequently lead to imitation ↔ Imitation by one's competitors\nimitation 同词+补出施动者\n【排除】A) 未提及\nPossible bankruptcy 录音从未提及，是凭\"风险=最坏结果\"的常识硬推，男方只点了\nimitation 一种风险。\nB) 未提及\nUnforeseen difficulties 太笼统，录音点的是具体的 imitation，没说\"意外难题\"，属无中\n生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\nConflicts within the company 借了通篇出现的 company 诱选，但录音从没提\"公司内部\n冲突\"，风险明确是外部的 imitation。\n借⽤原⽂：at all levels of the company", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What are the speakers mainly talking about?", "options": ["A) The job of an interpreter.", "B) The stress felt by professionals.", "C) The importance of language proficiency.", "D) The best way to effective communication."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题） The job of an interpreter.\n【定位】Today my guest is Dana Ivanovich, who has worked for the last 20 years as an interpreter.\n今天我的嘉宾是 Dana Ivanovich，过去 20 年她一直从事口译工作。\n【信号】开场介绍嘉宾身份即点题。主持人开场就用 who has worked for the last 20 years as an\ninterpreter 点出嘉宾是口译员，随后全程围绕口译展开，这种\"开头介绍身份+话题\"的句子\n是访谈类对话的主旨信号。\n【替换】who has worked for the last 20 years as an interpreter ↔ The job of an interpreter.\n身份引出职业话题\n【排除】B) 答非所问\n原文确实谈到 puts your short-term memory under intense stress 的压力，但那只是聊\n口译时的一个分支，The stress felt by professionals. 把局部当主旨，以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：puts your short-term memory under intense stress\nC) 答非所问\n原文借男士之口提到\"会多种语言不等于能做口译\"，反衬口译之专业，并未把\"语言能力的重\n要性\"当主题；The importance of language proficiency. 抓了个一闪而过的点冒充主旨。\n借⽤原⽂：many people think anyone who speaks more than one language can do it\nD) 未提及\n对话谈的是口译这一职业本身，从头到尾没有讨论\"怎样有效沟通\"，The best way to\neffective communication. 在原文找不到落点，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man think of Dana's profession?", "options": ["A) Promising.", "B) Admirable.", "C) Rewarding.", "D) Meaningful."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） Admirable.\n【定位】So I'm full of admiration for what you do.\n所以我对你所做的工作满怀钦佩。\n【信号】直白的评价用词 admiration。男士直接说 I'm full of admiration for what you do，\nadmiration 是明确的褒义评价词，态度题就抓这种第一人称、带感情色彩的直陈句。\n【替换】I'm full of admiration for what you do ↔ Admirable.\n名词 admiration 转形容词 admirable\n【排除】A) 未提及\nPromising.（有前途的）在男士的评价里没有依据，男士谈的是钦佩与这行被低估，未涉及\n前景，属无中生有。\nC) 未提及\nRewarding.（有回报的）原文男士并未提及，他强调的是\"钦佩\"与\"职业被低估\"，并没有说\n这份工作回报丰厚。\nD) 未提及\nMeaningful.（有意义的）同样在原文找不到对应，男士的评价词是 admiration，不是\"有\n意义\"，属于近义褒义词凑数。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does Dana say about the interpreters she knows?", "options": ["A) They all have a strong interest in language.", "B) They all have professional qualifications.", "C) They have all passed language proficiency tests.", "D) They have all studied cross-cultural differences."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They all have professional qualifications.\n【定位】There aren't any interpreters I know who don't have professional qualifications and training.\n我认识的口译员里，没有一个是不具备专业资格和培训的。\n【信号】双重否定即全称肯定。There aren't any interpreters I know who don't have\nprofessional qualifications 是\"没有...不...\"的双重否定，等于\"我认识的口译员都有专业资\n格\"，这种 no...without 结构是细节题常埋的全称信号。\n【替换】There aren't any interpreters I know who don't have professional qualifications and training ↔\nThey all have professional qualifications.\n双重否定改写为 all have\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey all have a strong interest in language.（都对语言有浓厚兴趣）原文 Dana 没说，\n她强调的是 professional qualifications and training，不是兴趣，属无中生有。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey have all passed language proficiency tests. 借用了第 5 题选项里 language\nproficiency 的字眼制造熟悉感，但原文说的是 professional qualifications and training\n（专业资格与培训），不是\"通过语言能力测试\"，把\"专业资格\"偷换成\"语言测试\"。\n借⽤原⽂：professional qualifications and training\nD) 未提及\nThey have all studied cross-cultural differences.（都研究过跨文化差异）原文完全没有\n出现，是凭\"口译涉及文化\"的联想编造，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What do most interpreters think of consecutive interpreting?", "options": ["A) It requires a much larger vocabulary.", "B) It attaches more importance to accuracy.", "C) It is more stressful than simultaneous interpreting.", "D) It puts one's long-term memory under more stress."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It is more stressful than simultaneous interpreting.\n【定位】Well, actually no. Most people in the business would agree that consecutive interpreting is the\nmore stressful.\n其实不是。业内大多数人都会同意，交替传译压力更大。\n【信号】actually no 转折后才是真意。男士先猜 simultaneous 更难，Dana 用 Well, actually no\n转折否定，紧接 Most people in the business would agree that consecutive\ninterpreting is the more stressful 才是结论；态度被纠正后的那句、配上 more stressful\n比较结构，正是答案句。\n【替换】consecutive interpreting is the more stressful ↔\nIt is more stressful than simultaneous interpreting.\n比较对象补全\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nIt requires a much larger vocabulary. 中\"词汇\"易让人联想原文 prepare technical\nexpressions，但那段讲的是 simultaneous 的事前准备，且原文并未说交替传译\"需要更大\n词汇量\"，属无中生有兼张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：prepare technical expressions\nB) 原词陷阱\nIt attaches more importance to accuracy. 蹭了原文 never translated word for word 给\n人的\"准确\"印象，但原文恰恰说交替传译\"不逐字译、要概括\"，谈的是处理方式而非\"更重准\n确度\"，方向被改。\n借⽤原⽂：never translated word for word\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt puts one's long-term memory under more stress. 盗用了原文 puts your short-term\nmemory under intense stress 里的 memory，但原文压的是 short-term memory（短时\n记忆），选项偷改成 long-term memory（长时记忆），一字之差正好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：puts your short-term memory under intense stress", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What is the long-held view about mothers' sleeping with newborn babies?", "options": ["A) It might affect mothers' health.", "B) It might disturb infants' sleep.", "C) It might increase the risk of infants' death.", "D) It might increase mothers' mental distress."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It might increase the risk of infants' death.\n【定位】Mothers have been warned for years that sleeping with their newborn infant is a bad idea,\nbecause it increases the risk that the baby might die unexpectedly during the night.\n多年来一直有人警告母亲，和新生婴儿同睡是个坏主意，因为这会增加婴儿在夜间意外死亡\n的风险。\n【信号】for years 对应题干 long-held。句首 Mothers have been warned for years 里的 for\nyears（多年来）正对题干的 long-held（长期持有的看法），这种『时间持续』信号标出\n了那条由来已久的旧观点，是答案句的定位标记。\n【替换】increases the risk that the baby might die unexpectedly during the night ↔\nIt might increase the risk of infants' death.\nthe baby might die = infants' death 名词化等值\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nIt might affect mothers' health. 借用了通篇高频词 mothers 来诱选，但本题问的旧观点\n针对的是婴儿死亡风险，『影响妈妈健康』是后文以色列研究的新发现、不是 long-held\nview，受害对象张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：negative consequences, not for the child, but for the mother\nB) 原词陷阱\nIt might disturb infants' sleep. 套用了原文 sleep 这一核心词，但原文恰恰说婴儿睡眠没\n变差（Infants...didn't appear to have worse sleep），且旧观点讲的是死亡风险而非扰乱\n婴儿睡眠，答非所问且与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Infants, on the other hand, didn't appear to have worse sleep\nD) 未提及\nIt might increase mothers' mental distress. 同样借 mothers 诱选，但原文从未说同睡增\n加母亲的『心理压力』，只说母亲睡眠变差；mental distress 属无中生有，且对象与旧观\n点不符。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do Israeli researchers' findings show?", "options": ["A) Mothers who breast-feed their babies have a harder time falling asleep.", "B) Mothers who sleep with their babies need a little more sleep each night.", "C) Sleeping patterns of mothers greatly affect their newborn babies' health.", "D) Sleeping with infants in the same room has a negative impact on mothers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Sleeping with infants in the same room has a negative impact on mothers.\n【定位】But now Israeli researchers are reporting that even sleeping in the same room can have\nnegative consequences, not for the child, but for the mother.\n但如今以色列研究者报告说，即使只是睡在同一个房间，也会带来负面影响——不是对孩\n子，而是对母亲。\n【信号】But now 转折后才是新发现。句首 But now 是强转折信号，前半句的旧观点（害婴儿）被\n推翻，转折之后 negative consequences, not for the child, but for the mother 才是以\n色列研究的真正发现，转折后才是落点。\n【替换】negative consequences, not for the child, but for the mother ↔\nSleeping with infants in the same room has a negative impact on mothers.\nnegative consequences for the mother = negative impact on mothers\neven sleeping in the same room ↔ Sleeping with infants in the same room\nsame room 两端原义保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nMothers who breast-feed their babies have a harder time falling asleep. 套用原文出\n现过的 breast-feeding 一词，但原文说的是把哺乳因素排除后结论仍成立（even taking\ninto account that many of the women...were breast-feeding），从未说哺乳的妈妈更难\n入睡，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：even taking into account that many of the women in the study were breast-feeding\ntheir babies\nB) 与原文相反\nMothers who sleep with their babies need a little more sleep each night. 盗用\nsleep、each night 等词，但原文说同房母亲睡得更差、睡眠时间更短（were awake\napproximately 20 minutes longer per night），不是『需要更多睡眠』，方向与原文相\n反。\n借⽤原⽂：had poorer sleep than mothers whose baby slept elsewhere\nC) 张冠李戴\nSleeping patterns of mothers greatly affect their newborn babies' health. 盗用\nmothers、newborn 等词，但研究恰恰发现婴儿睡眠未受影响、受害的是母亲，把影响对\n象反向安到婴儿健康上，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：not for the child, but for the mother", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the American Academy of Pediatrics recommend mothers do?", "options": ["A) Change their sleep patterns to adapt to their newborn babies'.", "B) Sleep in the same room but not in the same bed as their babies.", "C) Sleep in the same house but not in the same room as their babies.", "D) Take precautions to reduce the risk of sudden infant death syndrome."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Sleep in the same room but not in the same bed as their babies.\n【定位】Right now, to reduce the risk of sudden infant death syndrome, the American Academy of\nPediatrics recommends that mothers not sleep in the same bed as their babies, but sleep in the\nsame room.\n目前，为降低婴儿猝死综合征的风险，美国儿科学会建议母亲不要和婴儿睡在同一张床上，\n但可以睡在同一个房间。\n【信号】recommends 建议信号直接领出对策。句中 the American Academy of Pediatrics\nrecommends 与题干 recommend 同根对应，建议词后接的 not sleep in the same\nbed...but sleep in the same room 就是要找的具体做法，建议信号是定位灵魂。\n【替换】not sleep in the same bed as their babies, but sleep in the same room ↔\nSleep in the same room but not in the same bed as their babies.\nnot bed but room = same room but not same bed 仅语序对调\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nChange their sleep patterns to adapt to their newborn babies'. 套用了原文末尾出现的\nsleep patterns 一词，但那是说『父亲的睡眠模式可能也是干扰源』（their sleep patterns\ncould also be causing the sleep disruptions），并非建议母亲去改作息迁就婴儿，答非\n所问。\n借⽤原⽂：their sleep patterns could also be causing the sleep disruptions for mums\nC) 与原文相反\nSleep in the same house but not in the same room as their babies. 与正确项只差几个\n空间词，盗用了 same room 的句式却把建议改成『同屋不同房』，恰与原文 sleep in the\nsame room（要同房）相反，是相近选项里的空间词偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：but sleep in the same room\nD) 原词陷阱\nTake precautions to reduce the risk of sudden infant death syndrome. 把原文原样出\n现的 reduce the risk of sudden infant death syndrome 整条搬来诱选，但那是建议的\n『目的/原因』，不是建议母亲做的『具体动作』；题干问 do what，该项只是笼统说采取\n预防、答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：to reduce the risk of sudden infant death syndrome", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do we learn from the report?", "options": ["A) A lot of native languages have already died out in the US.", "B) The US ranks first in the number of endangered languages.", "C) The efforts to preserve Indian languages have proved fruitless.", "D) More money is needed to record the native languages in the US."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） A lot of native languages have already died out in the US.\n【定位】The US has already lost more than a third of the native languages that existed before\nEuropean colonization and the remaining 192 are classed by UNESCO as ranging between\nunsafe and extinct.\n美国已经失去了欧洲殖民之前存在的本土语言中三分之一以上，而剩下的 192 种被联合国教\n科文组织划为从不安全到濒临灭绝不等。\n【信号】开头既成事实句。录音第一句就用 has already lost more than a third 直陈既成事实，\nalready 加完成态是定位答案句的信号——篇章独白常在开头点出核心事实，这一句正是承\n载本题答案的事实句。\n【替换】has already lost more than a third of the native languages ↔\nA lot of native languages have already died out in the US\n已失去等于已消亡\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 The US ranks first in the number of endangered languages. 盗用录音里 the\nremaining 192 这个数字制造熟悉感，但录音只说剩 192 种被划为濒危，从未说美国濒危\n语言数量排名第一，ranks first 是凭空捏造的排名。\n借⽤原⽂：the remaining 192 are classed by UNESCO\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 The efforts to preserve Indian languages have proved fruitless. 与录音相反：录\n音借发言人说 We are making progress（我们正在取得进展），明确肯定保护工作有成\n效，C 却说努力毫无成效，极性正好反了。\n借⽤原⽂：We are making progress\nD) 答非所问\n选项 More money is needed to record the native languages in the US. 借用录音\nmoney needs to be spent 制造熟悉感，但录音强调钱要花在复兴而非记录上，且这句是\n发言人呼吁经费的目的（对应第 13 题），并非本题问的从报道中得知的总体事实，答非所\n问。\n借⽤原⽂：money needs to be spent on revitalizing languages, not just documenting them", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "For what purpose does Fred Nawusky appeal for more funding?", "options": ["A) To set up more language schools.", "B) To document endangered languages.", "C) To educate native American children.", "D) To revitalise America's native languages."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （目的题） To revitalise America's native languages.\n【定位】We are making progress, but money needs to be spent on revitalizing languages, not just\ndocumenting them.\n我们正在取得进展，但钱需要花在复兴语言上，而不只是记录它们。\n【信号】转折 but 加对比。答案藏在转折 but 之后，且用 not just documenting them 把记录与复\n兴对比，强调钱要花在复兴上——转折后加 not just A but B 的对比，正是锁定真正目的的\n信号。\n【替换】money needs to be spent on revitalizing languages ↔ To revitalise America's native languages\n动词同义直对\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n选项 To set up more language schools. 借用第二段 emerging schools（新兴学校）制\n造关联，但学校是后文谈语言传承时提到的，并非发言人呼吁经费的目的，且原文没说要用\n钱多建学校，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：progress is being made through emerging schools\nB) 与原文相反\n选项 To document endangered languages. 盗用录音 documenting them 这个原词，但\n录音用 not just 把记录明确排除为应优先的目的，钱不该只花在记录上，选 B 等于选了被\n否定的那一半。\n借⽤原⽂：not just documenting them\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 To educate native American children. 借第二段 if you teach children when they\nare young 制造熟悉感，但教孩子是发言人谈语言如何延续时说的，不是他呼吁经费的目\n的，张冠李戴到了经费用途上。\n借⽤原⽂：if you teach children when they are young", "points": ["听力·篇章", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What is the historical cause of the decline in American Indian Languages?", "options": ["A) The US government's policy of Americanising Indian children.", "B) The failure of American Indian languages to gain an official status.", "C) The US government's unwillingness to spend money educating Indians.", "D) The long-time isolation of American Indians from the outside world."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The US government's policy of Americanising Indian children.\n【定位】The decline in American Indian languages has its historical roots. In the mid-19th century, the\nUS government adopted a policy of Americanizing Indian children by removing them from\ntheir homes and culture.\n美国印第安语言的衰落有其历史根源。19 世纪中期，美国政府采取了一项把印第安儿童从\n家庭和文化中带走、对其进行美国化的政策。\n【信号】has its historical roots。录音用 has its historical roots 显式引出历史根源，紧接着的\nthe US government adopted a policy of Americanizing Indian children 就是原因句\n——roots 这一因果信号词后面跟的内容即答案所在。\n【替换】the US government adopted a policy of Americanizing Indian children ↔\nThe US government's policy of Americanising Indian children.\n近乎原句对应\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项 The failure of American Indian languages to gain an official status. 说语言未获官\n方地位，录音通篇未提官方地位一事，属凭空添加。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 The US government's unwillingness to spend money educating Indians. 盗用录\n音前文 money needs to be spent 的花钱话题制造熟悉感，但录音说的是政府主动同化、\n带走儿童，从未说政府不愿花钱教育印第安人，且这与历史原因无关。\n借⽤原⽂：money needs to be spent on revitalizing languages\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 The long-time isolation of American Indians from the outside world. 与录音方向\n相反：录音说政府把儿童从家庭文化中带走、强行同化（让他们融入而非隔绝），D 却说长\n期与外界隔绝，正好反了。\n借⽤原⽂：removing them from their homes and culture", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the speaker say about television?", "options": ["A) It is being utilised to teach native languages.", "B) It tells traditional stories during family time.", "C) It speeds up the extinction of native languages.", "D) It is widely used in language immersion schools."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It speeds up the extinction of native languages.\n【定位】Another challenge to language survival is television. It has brought English into homes, and\npushed out traditional storytelling and family time together, accelerating the extinction of\nnative languages.\n语言存续的另一项挑战是电视。它把英语带进家庭，挤掉了传统讲故事和家人相处的时间，\n加速了本土语言的灭绝。\n【信号】challenge 加 accelerating。录音先用 Another challenge to language survival is\ntelevision 给电视定了负面基调，再以 accelerating the extinction 点出后果——\nchallenge 与 accelerating 这两个负面词共同标定答案句。\n【替换】accelerating the extinction of native languages ↔ It speeds up the extinction of native languages.\n加速同义改写\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 It is being utilised to teach native languages. 把电视说成教语言的正面工具，与录\n音相反：录音说电视把英语带进家庭、加速本土语言灭绝，是负面角色，并未说用它来教本\n土语言。\n借⽤原⽂：Another challenge to language survival is television\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 It tells traditional stories during family time. 盗用录音 traditional storytelling\nand family time 的原词，但录音说电视挤掉了传统讲故事和家人相处时间，B 却说电视在\n讲传统故事，把被挤掉的对象说成电视的行为，意思反了。\n借⽤原⽂：pushed out traditional storytelling and family time together\nD) 张冠李戴\n选项 It is widely used in language immersion schools. 把第二段谈语言学校（emerging\nschools）的内容安到电视头上，录音从未说电视广泛用于沉浸式语言学校，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：Such schools have become a model in Hawaii", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "How does unemployment insurance help the unemployed?", "options": ["A) It pays them up to half of their previous wages while they look for work.", "B) It covers their mortgage payments and medical expenses for 99 weeks.", "C) It pays their living expenses until they find employment again.", "D) It provides them with the basic necessities of everyday life."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It pays them up to half of their previous wages while they look for work.\n【定位】It pays laid-off workers up to half of their previous wages while they look for work.\n（失业保险）在被裁员工找工作期间，向他们支付最高达原先工资一半的钱。\n【信号】紧跟制度名后的“It pays…”界定句。答案句是制度被点名后立刻给出的定义句 It pays laid-\noff workers up to half of their previous wages while they look for work.，以 It pays…\n直接说明它发什么、发多少，这种“主语+pays+具体内容”的界定句就是细节题的落点。\n【替换】It pays laid-off workers up to half of their previous wages ↔\nIt pays them up to half of their previous wages\nlaid-off workers 概括为代词 them，其余近乎原义\nwhile they look for work ↔ while they look for work\n限定条件原义对应\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nIt covers their mortgage payments and medical expenses for 99 weeks. 里 mortgage\npayments、medical（药）确在录音别处出现，但那是 Rosen 个人的开销与困境，并非失\n业保险的界定内容；99 weeks 与“支付一半工资”更是无关，属把分散原词拼成假信息。\n借⽤原⽂：He's cut back on medications and he no longer helps support his disabled mother.\nC) 偷换概念\nIt pays their living expenses until they find employment again. 方向对，但录音明确是\nup to half of their previous wages（最高一半工资），并非覆盖全部生活开销；C 抹掉了\n关键限额“一半”，把精确条件偷换成笼统说法。\n借⽤原⽂：up to half of their previous wages\nD) 未提及\nIt provides them with the basic necessities of everyday life. 听上去合情合理，但录音从\n未说失业保险提供“基本生活必需品”，它发的是按比例的现金（一半工资），D 属凭常识脑\n补、原文未提。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What is local director Elizabeth Walsh of the Bucks County Careerlink doing?", "options": ["A) Creating jobs for the huge army of unemployed workers.", "B) Providing training and guidance for unemployed workers.", "C) Convincing local lawmakers to extend unemployment benefits.", "D) Raising funds to help those having no unemployment insurance."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Providing training and guidance for unemployed workers.\n【定位】Local director Elizabeth Walsh says they provide training and guidance to help unemployed\nworkers find local job opportunities.\n本地负责人 Elizabeth Walsh 说，他们提供培训与指导，帮失业工人找到本地的工作机会。\n【信号】人名 + says + 其动作的应答式落点。答案句以人名直接领出 Elizabeth Walsh says they\nprovide training and guidance，人名后紧跟的“says + 做什么”就是该人物对应的答案信\n号，谁说的归谁。\n【替换】they provide training and guidance ↔ Providing training and guidance\n动词 provide 转动名词 Providing，核心词原词保留\nhelp unemployed workers find local job opportunities ↔ for unemployed workers\n服务对象原义对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nCreating jobs for the huge army of unemployed workers. 用了原文反复出现的 jobs、\nunemployed workers，听上去顺耳；但 Walsh 做的是提供培训与指导、撮合岗位与求职\n者，“创造岗位”是后文 Scott Petri 的投资主张才能带来的，非她所为。\n借⽤原⽂：provide training and guidance to help unemployed workers find local job opportunities\nC) 张冠李戴\nConvincing local lawmakers to extend unemployment benefits. 中 extend\nunemployment benefits 确在录音出现，但那是 Rosen 创立的 Ninety-Niners Union 的\n目标，不是 Walsh 在做的事，属把别人的事安到本题人物身上。\n借⽤原⽂：Their goal is to convince lawmakers to extend unemployment benefits.\nD) 未提及\nRaising funds to help those having no unemployment insurance. 看似也是救助失业\n者，但录音里 Walsh 没有筹款之举，“为无保险者筹款”全篇未出现，属凭印象脑补。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does Pennsylvania state representative Scott Petri say is the best way to help the long-term unemployed?", "options": ["A) To offer them loans they need to start their own businesses.", "B) To allow them to postpone their monthly mortgage payments.", "C) To create more jobs by encouraging private investments in local companies.", "D) To encourage big businesses to hire back workers with government subsidies."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To create more jobs by encouraging private investments in local companies.\n【定位】He thinks the best way to help the long-term unemployed is to allow private citizens to invest\nin local companies that can create more jobs.\n他认为帮助长期失业者最好的办法，是允许私人公民投资本地公司，从而创造更多就业\n岗位。\n【信号】the best way … is to … 主张落点句。答案句以 He thinks the best way to help the\nlong-term unemployed is to allow private citizens to invest in local companies that\ncan create more jobs. 给出明确主张，the best way…is to… 这种带评价的句式正是“某人\n认为最佳办法”的答案信号。\n【替换】allow private citizens to invest in local companies ↔\nencouraging private investments in local companies\n动词 invest 名词化为 investments，allow≈encouraging\nthat can create more jobs ↔ To create more jobs\n目的从句转不定式，原义对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTo offer them loans they need to start their own businesses. 中创业贷款听似帮扶失业\n者，但 Petri 主张的是吸引私人投资本地公司，从未提及给个人贷款创业，属凭常识脑补。\nB) 原词陷阱\nTo allow them to postpone their monthly mortgage payments. 里 monthly\nmortgage payments 确在录音末尾出现，但那是讲 Rosen 仍要还房贷的处境，不是 Petri\n提出的办法；“缓交房贷”是 Petri 的主张里没有的。\n借⽤原⽂：time that Rosen says still requires him to buy food and make monthly\nmortgage payments\nD) 与原文相反\nTo encourage big businesses to hire back workers with government subsidies. 也讲\n创造就业，但 Petri 明确说政府没钱（governments simply do not have enough\nmoney），主张靠私人投资而非政府补贴；D 把资金来源反过来，与原文相左。\n借⽤原⽂：governments simply do not have enough money to extend unemployment insurance", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What did Pen Hadow and his team do in the Arctic Ocean?", "options": ["A) They measured the depths of sea water.", "B) They analyzed the water content.", "C) They explored the ocean floor.", "D) They investigated the ice."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They investigated the ice.\n【定位】Earlier this year, British explorer Pen Hadow and his team trekked for three months across\nthe frozen Arctic Ocean, taking measurements and recording observations about the ice.\n今年早些时候，英国探险家 Pen Hadow 和他的团队在结冰的北冰洋上跋涉了三个月，对冰\n进行测量并记录观察数据。\n【信号】人名出现处即答案区。录音开篇就点名 British explorer Pen Hadow and his team，和题\n干问的是同一拨人；紧跟其后的 taking measurements and recording observations\nabout the ice 正是他们的动作。人名一出现、其后紧跟的动作就是答案句。\n【替换】taking measurements and recording observations about the ice ↔ investigated the ice\n测量记录冰=研究冰（概括）\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 taking measurements（测量）一词，A 的 measured 听上去就是它；但录音测\n的是“冰”，A 却说测 the depths of sea water（海水深度）。盗了 measure 这个原词，却\n把测量对象换成了海水深度，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：taking measurements and recording observations about the ice\nB) 未提及\n录音通篇没提分析海水成分这回事，analyzed the water content 是看到“海洋”就联想的科\n研常见动作。属录音未提及的内容。\nC) 答非所问\nC 说 explored the ocean floor（探索海底），但录音里这队人研究的是 the ice（冰），\n不是海床。explore 是常见科考动词，听感顺，但研究对象与录音相反方向、对不上。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does the report say about the Arctic region?", "options": ["A) Eighty percent of the ice disappears in summertime.", "B) Most of the ice was accumulated over the past centuries.", "C) The ice ensures the survival of many endangered species.", "D) The ice decrease is more evident than previously thought."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The ice decrease is more evident than previously thought.\n【定位】But the recent measurements show the loss is more pronounced than previously thought.\n但是最近的测量显示，（冰的）损失比先前认为的更显著。\n【信号】转折 But 之后才是真意。本句以 But 起头，前半句 Satellite observations and\nsubmarine service over the past few years have shown less ice 是铺垫，转折 But 之后\n的 the loss is more pronounced than previously thought 才是报道真正强调的结论。转\n折词后即答案落点。\n【替换】the loss is more pronounced than previously thought ↔\nThe ice decrease is more evident than previously thought\npronounced=evident、loss=decrease 同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第 4 段确有 roughly 80 percent loss of ice cover on the Arctic Ocean in ten\nyears，A 的 Eighty percent 就是它；但录音说的是“十年内损失八成”，A 却说成“夏天消失\n八成”（disappears in summertime）。盗了 80% 这个数字，时间范围却被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：roughly 80 percent loss of ice cover on the Arctic Ocean in ten years\nB) 未提及\n录音通篇只讲冰在快速消失，从未说冰是“过去几个世纪累积”而来。B 是凭“厚冰应该是长年\n攒出来的”常识脑补，录音无对应。\nC) 未提及\nC 把话题引到 endangered species（濒危物种的存活），但录音从头到尾没谈冰对物种的\n保护作用，整条是录音之外的延伸。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does Cambridge scientist Peter Wadhams say in his study?", "options": ["A) Arctic ice is a major source of the world's fresh water.", "B) The melting Arctic ice has drowned many coastal cities.", "C) The decline of Arctic ice is irreversible.", "D) Arctic ice is essential to human survival."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The decline of Arctic ice is irreversible.\n【定位】Cambridge scientist Peter Wadhams, who's been measuring and monitoring the Arctic since\n1971, says the decline is irreversible.\n剑桥科学家 Peter Wadhams 自 1971 年以来一直在测量和监测北极，他说这种衰退是不可\n逆的。\n【信号】姓名 + says 直接引出观点。录音用 Cambridge scientist Peter Wadhams… says 直接点\n名并引出他的判断 the decline is irreversible。题干问的就是这个人 say 什么，姓名加\nsays 之后的内容即答案句。\n【替换】the decline is irreversible ↔ The decline of Arctic ice is irreversible\n近乎原句、仅补出 decline 指向\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音没说北极冰是世界淡水的主要来源。a major source of… fresh water 是凭“冰=淡水”\n常识的推想，录音无对应。\nB) 过度推断\n录音只讲冰在加速消失（第 6 段讲恶性循环），从未说已经 drowned many coastal cities\n（淹了很多沿海城市）。这是“冰化→海平面升→淹城”的常识链条脑补，录音没说已发生。\nD) 张冠李戴\n“对人类至关重要”这层意思更接近第 8 段 Summercorn 说的 The Arctic sea ice holds a\ncentral position in the earth's climate system，而题干问的是 Wadhams 在他研究里说\n的。把别人对气候系统的话安到 Wadhams 头上，属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：The Arctic sea ice holds a central position in the earth's climate system", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "How does Peter Wadhams view climate change?", "options": ["A) It will do a lot of harm to mankind.", "B) There is no easy way to understand it.", "C) It will advance nuclear technology.", "D) There is no easy technological solution to it."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） There is no easy technological solution to it.\n【定位】Wadhams says there is no easy technological fix to climate change.\nWadhams 说，气候变化没有简单的技术解决办法。\n【信号】姓名 + says 引出表态。结尾段 Wadhams says there is no easy technological fix to\nclimate change，再次以姓名 + says 引出他对气候变化的看法。题干问 Wadhams 的观\n点，这句即答案。\n【替换】no easy technological fix to climate change ↔ There is no easy technological solution to it\nfix=solution 同义、it 指代 climate change\n【排除】A) 未提及\n“气候变化对人类有大害”虽符合常识，但录音里 Wadhams 这段说的是“没有简单技术办\n法”，并未对气候变化下“有大害”这个判断。属凭常识脑补、录音未明说。\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 与正确项 D 都以 no easy 开头，录音确有 no easy technological fix；但 B 后接 way to\nunderstand it（难以理解），录音说的是难以“技术解决”，不是难“理解”。盗了 no easy 的\n句式，把 fix 偷换成 understand。\n借⽤原⽂：there is no easy technological fix to climate change\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音末句确实念到 embracing nuclear power（拥抱核能），C 的 nuclear technology 就\n是勾这个词；但录音是把核能列为“替代化石燃料的选项之一”，绝非说“气候变化会推进核技\n术”。盗了 nuclear 原词，硬安了一个不存在的因果。\n借⽤原⽂：embracing nuclear power", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What is the new study about?", "options": ["A) The reason why New Zealand children seem to have better self-control.", "B) The relation between children's self-control and their future success.", "C) The health problems of children raised by a single parent.", "D) The deciding factor in children's academic performance."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） The relation between children's self-control and their future success.\n【定位】Now, a new study that began with about 1,000 children in New Zealand has tracked how a\nchild's low self-control can predict poor health, money troubles and even a criminal record in\ntheir adult years.\n如今，一项以新西兰约 1000 名儿童为起点的新研究，追踪了儿童的低自控力如何能预示其\n成年后的健康不佳、经济困难乃至犯罪记录。\n【信号】开头点题句 a new study...has tracked how，直接交代研究干什么。篇章独白的主旨多\n在开头点出。这里 a new study that began with about 1,000 children in New Zealand\nhas tracked how a child's low self-control can predict poor health, money troubles\nand even a criminal record in their adult years 用 has tracked how 明说研究在追踪\"自\n控力如何预测成年后果\"，正是全篇主题句。\n【替换】how a child's low self-control can predict poor health, money troubles and even a criminal record in\ntheir adult years\n↔ The relation between children's self-control and their future success.\n\"自控力预测成年后果\"被概括成\"自控力与未来的关系\"\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 had the most health problems 和单亲（raising a child as a single\nparent），C 把这两个原词拼成 The health problems of children raised by a single\nparent.，但这只是研究中的一个结果片段，不是研究主题；用真实出现过的词诱你\"听到就\n选\"。\n借⽤原⽂：health problems in their thirties\nD) 未提及\nThe deciding factor in children's academic performance. 把话题缩到\"学业表现的决定\n性因素\"，但全篇讲的是健康、经济、犯罪等成年结果，根本没说自控力是学业的\"决定性因\n素\"，既偷换了话题面又把程度说满。\nA) 原词陷阱\nThe reason why New Zealand children seem to have better self-control. 借了原文的\nNew Zealand、self-control 原词，但录音从没说\"新西兰孩子自控更好\"，更没解释其原\n因；研究只是恰好在新西兰做，A 是把地名细节硬编成一个不存在的主题。\n借⽤原⽂：about 1,000 children in New Zealand", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the study seem to show?", "options": ["A) Children raised by single parents will have a hard time in their thirties.", "B) Those with a criminal record mostly come from single parent families.", "C) Parents must learn to exercise self-control in front of their children.", "D) Lack of self-control in parents is a disadvantage for their children."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Lack of self-control in parents is a disadvantage for their children.\n【定位】So it looks as though self-control is something that in one generation can disadvantage the\nnext generation.\n所以看起来，自我控制力是一种会让上一代殃及下一代的东西。\n【信号】结论信号 So it looks as though，研究者收束式的总判断。So it looks as though self-\ncontrol is something that in one generation can disadvantage the next generation\n由 So it looks as though 引出，是研究者对前文一连串现象的总结性判断，这种\"所以看\n来……\"句正是研究\"显示出\"的核心结论。\n【替换】self-control is something that in one generation can disadvantage the next generation ↔\nLack of self-control in parents is a disadvantage for their children.\n\"上一代殃及下一代\"换成\"父母缺自控不利于孩子\"\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音里 more likely to have a criminal record 和单亲都出现过，B 借这两个词拼成 Those\nwith a criminal record mostly come from single parent families.，但原文说的是\"低自\n控者更可能有犯罪记录\"，方向是\"自控差→可能犯罪\"，B 反过来说\"有犯罪记录的大多来自\n单亲\"，既倒置因果又用 mostly 夸大。\n借⽤原⽂：more likely to have a criminal record\nA) 张冠李戴\nChildren raised by single parents will have a hard time in their thirties. 借了 in their\nthirties 和单亲两个原词，但录音说的是\"低自控的孩子三十多岁时问题最多\"，主语是低自\n控者、不是\"单亲养大的孩子\"，A 张冠李戴换了主体。\n借⽤原⽂：had the most health problems in their thirties\nC) 未提及\nParents must learn to exercise self-control in front of their children. 借了 self-\ncontrol、parents、children 原词，但研究只是\"显示\"自控会跨代影响，从没说父母\"必须\n当着孩子练自控\"这种 must 式的行为要求，属凭空添加的建议。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does Moffitt say is the good news from their study?", "options": ["A) Self-control can be improved through education.", "B) Self-control can improve one's financial situation.", "C) Self-control problems may be detected early in children.", "D) Self-control problems will diminish as one grows up."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Self-control can be improved through education.\n【定位】But the good news is that Moffitt says self-control can be taught by parents, and through\nschool curricula that have proved to be effective.\n但好消息是，Moffitt 说自我控制力可以由父母传授，也能通过已被证明有效的学校课程来\n培养。\n【信号】转折 + 原词信号 But the good news is，直接对上题干 good news。But the good\nnews is that Moffitt says self-control can be taught by parents, and through school\ncurricula 既有转折词 But，又把题干的 the good news is 原样念出，双重信号锁定这句就\n是答案句。\n【替换】self-control can be taught by parents, and through school curricula ↔\nSelf-control can be improved through education.\n\"可被父母教、靠学校课程\"概括成\"可经教育改善\"\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nfinancial 在录音里反复出现（improved their financial situation），B 用 Self-control\ncan improve one's financial situation. 勾你\"听到 financial 就选\"。但题目问的是 good\nnews，录音 good news 那句讲的是\"能被教育培养\"，没说\"改善经济\"是好消息；财务改善\n是别处的内容，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：improved their financial situation\nD) 与原文相反\nSelf-control problems will diminish as one grows up. 借了 self-control 原词，但好消息\n句的意思是要靠 taught by parents, and through school curricula 后天培养，恰恰说明\n它不会自动变好；D 的\"随长大自然减弱\"与原文方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：self-control can be taught by parents\nC) 答非所问\nSelf-control problems may be detected early in children. 看似呼应录音里\"从很小年龄\n就观察自控\"（From a very early age），但题目问的是 good news，good news 那句讲\n的是\"能被教育培养\"，不是\"能早发现\"；C 把别处细节移花接木到本题。\n借⽤原⽂：From a very early age, some children exhibit better self-control than others", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) avoiding", "B) benefits", "C) highlight", "D) illustrates", "E) impression", "F) improves", "G) inquiring", "H) perfectly", "I) positive", "J) prevail", "K) primarily", "L) prompt", "M) specifications", "N) strapping", "O) typical"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) strapping\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 of 后接动名词\n【依据】strap on 穿上（旱冰鞋）\nstrap on one's roller-skates 指'系紧、穿上旱冰鞋'，与 on your roller-skates 和'一想到就\n微笑'的语境吻合。\n【竞争词】A) avoiding 避免，与'微笑'矛盾\navoiding on your roller-skates 不通，且'避免'是消极义，与 brings a smile 矛盾。\nG) inquiring 询问，语义完全不通\ninquiring 是'询问'，与穿旱冰鞋毫无关系。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 of 后接动名词"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) avoiding", "B) benefits", "C) highlight", "D) illustrates", "E) impression", "F) improves", "G) inquiring", "H) perfectly", "I) positive", "J) prevail", "K) primarily", "L) prompt", "M) specifications", "N) strapping", "O) typical"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) positive\n形容词\n【词性槽】a 与名词 attitude 之间填形容词\n【依据】positive attitude 积极态度\n上文说喜欢轮滑、它是很好的运动，对它的态度自然是 positive，positive attitude 也是高\n频搭配。\n【竞争词】G) inquiring 好问的，对象不符\ninquiring attitude 是'爱探究的态度'，与'喜爱某项运动'的语境不符。\nO) typical 典型的，语义偏离\ntypical attitude 是'典型态度'，文中强调喜爱而非'典型'。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a 与名词 attitude 之间填形容"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) avoiding", "B) benefits", "C) highlight", "D) illustrates", "E) impression", "F) improves", "G) inquiring", "H) perfectly", "I) positive", "J) prevail", "K) primarily", "L) prompt", "M) specifications", "N) strapping", "O) typical"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) illustrates\n动词\n【词性槽】单数主语缺三单谓语，后接宾语\n【依据】说明、体现三种成分\nillustrate 意为'说明、例证'，'这段对轮滑的描述说明了态度的三种成分'与下文逐一介绍三\n成分吻合。\n【竞争词】F) improves 改善，与'说明'语义不符\nimproves the three components 是'改善三种成分'，文中并非改善、而是举例说明。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— improves 冒充 illustrates。improves 同是三单动词，\n但'改善成分'偏离'举例说明三成分'的原意。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·单数主语缺三单谓语，后接宾语"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) avoiding", "B) benefits", "C) highlight", "D) illustrates", "E) impression", "F) improves", "G) inquiring", "H) perfectly", "I) positive", "J) prevail", "K) primarily", "L) prompt", "M) specifications", "N) strapping", "O) typical"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) highlight\n动词\n【词性槽】复数主语缺原形谓语，后接宾语\n【依据】凸显情感成分\nhighlight the affective component 意为'凸显情感成分'，'这些喜爱的感受凸显了态度的\n情感成分'语义贴切。\n【竞争词】J) prevail 不及物，不能接宾语\nprevail 后不能直接跟 the component 作宾语，结构不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·复数主语缺原形谓语，后接宾语"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) avoiding", "B) benefits", "C) highlight", "D) illustrates", "E) impression", "F) improves", "G) inquiring", "H) perfectly", "I) positive", "J) prevail", "K) primarily", "L) prompt", "M) specifications", "N) strapping", "O) typical"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) benefits\n名词\n【词性槽】the+名词修饰+空+that，缺中心名词\n【依据】health benefits 固定搭配\nhealth benefits 是高频搭配，指活动'能带来的健康益处'，与 the activity can bring 完全\n吻合。\n【竞争词】C) highlight 亮点，不与 health 搭配\nhighlight 作名词是'最精彩处'，没有 health highlight 的说法。\nE) impression 印象，对象不符\nimpression 是人对事物的主观印象，不是活动'带来'的东西。\nM) specifications 规格参数，语义不搭\nspecifications 指规格/参数，与健康无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the+名词修饰+空+that，缺中心名"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) avoiding", "B) benefits", "C) highlight", "D) illustrates", "E) impression", "F) improves", "G) inquiring", "H) perfectly", "I) positive", "J) prevail", "K) primarily", "L) prompt", "M) specifications", "N) strapping", "O) typical"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) prompt\n动词\n【词性槽】复数主语缺谓语，后接 us to do\n【依据】prompt sb to do 促使某人做\nprompt us to go outside 意为'促使我们外出'，正是态度的'行为成分'在起作用。\n【竞争词】C) highlight 突出，不接 sb to do\nhighlight 是'强调某事物'，不能说 highlight us to go。\nJ) prevail 不及物，不能带宾语\nprevail 通常不及物，后面不能直接接 us to go。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·复数主语缺谓语，后接 us to do"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) avoiding", "B) benefits", "C) highlight", "D) illustrates", "E) impression", "F) improves", "G) inquiring", "H) perfectly", "I) positive", "J) prevail", "K) primarily", "L) prompt", "M) specifications", "N) strapping", "O) typical"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) impression\n名词\n【词性槽】the+空+that 同位语从句，缺名词\n【依据】leave sb with the impression that\nleave you with the impression that… 是固定表达'给你留下…的印象'，impression 能接\nthat 同位语从句。\n【竞争词】M) specifications 规格说明，搭配语义双不符\nspecifications 是'规格参数'，既不与 leave…with 搭配，也不能引出'三种成分总是协同'这类\n印象。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the+空+that 同位语从句，缺名词"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) avoiding", "B) benefits", "C) highlight", "D) illustrates", "E) impression", "F) improves", "G) inquiring", "H) perfectly", "I) positive", "J) prevail", "K) primarily", "L) prompt", "M) specifications", "N) strapping", "O) typical"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) perfectly\n副词\n【词性槽】修饰 work together 的副词\n【依据】完美地配合\n下一句 They don't; sometimes they clash 是转折，说明上句假设的是'三者总是完美协\n同'，故用 perfectly。\n【竞争词】K) primarily 主要地，与转折不呼应\nprimarily 是'主要地'，'主要地一起作用'与下文'其实会冲突'的反差不自然，只有 perfectly 才托\n得出转折。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— primarily 冒充 perfectly。两者都是副词、都能接在 work\ntogether 后，但只有 perfectly 能与下文'其实会冲突'形成反差。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·修饰 work together 的副词"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) avoiding", "B) benefits", "C) highlight", "D) illustrates", "E) impression", "F) improves", "G) inquiring", "H) perfectly", "I) positive", "J) prevail", "K) primarily", "L) prompt", "M) specifications", "N) strapping", "O) typical"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) avoiding\n动词\n【词性槽】与 eating 并列的动名词\n【依据】eating 与 avoiding 对举\n句意是'吃 pizza 还是不吃（避免）它'，avoiding it 与 eating pizza 正好相对。\n【竞争词】G) inquiring 询问，与吃/不吃无关\ninquiring it 不通，inquire 是'询问'，与'吃或避免'的对立无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·与 eating 并列的动名词"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) avoiding", "B) benefits", "C) highlight", "D) illustrates", "E) impression", "F) improves", "G) inquiring", "H) perfectly", "I) positive", "J) prevail", "K) primarily", "L) prompt", "M) specifications", "N) strapping", "O) typical"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) prevail\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 may 后接原形\n【依据】知识成分占上风\nprevail 意为'占上风、占主导'，'此时知识成分可能占上风，于是选更健康的一餐'语义\n连贯。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 may 后接原形"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Not many young people eligible for voting are interested in local or national elections these days.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】可投票年轻人 + 选举\n【定位】It is also troubling that voting rates among our youngest eligible voters—18- to 24-year-olds\n—are way down: Little more than one in four now go to the polls, even in national elections,\ncompared with almost twice that many when 18-year-olds were first given the vote.\n同样令人不安的是，我们最年轻的有投票资格者——18 到 24 岁——的投票率严重下滑：\n哪怕在全国大选中，现在去投票的也只比四分之一多一点，约为 18 岁刚获选举权时的\n一半。\n【改写】Not many young people eligible for voting ↔\nour youngest eligible voters—18- to 24-year-olds—are way down\n数量极少 ↔ 投票率严重下滑\nare interested in local or national elections ↔ go to the polls, even in national elections\n感兴趣 ↔ 去投票 的行为化改写\n【辨邻】 J J 段讲学生 viewed politics with suspicion and distaste，属于态度描写；题干要的\n是 选举参与度低 的事实数据，与 J 段不对应。\nMost politicians are kind of crooked(不诚实的)", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Parents are concerned that their children may get involved in criminal offences once they reach their teens.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】父母担心孩子犯罪\n【定位】No wonder parents worry their own kids might spin out of control once they hit the turbulent\nwaters of adolescence.\n难怪父母会担心自家孩子一旦进入波涛汹涌的青春期就会失控。\n【改写】Parents are concerned ↔ parents worry\nconcerned ↔ worry 同义词替换\ntheir children may get involved in criminal offences ↔ their own kids might spin out of control\n犯罪 ↔ 失控 的上下位改写\nonce they reach their teens ↔ once they hit the turbulent waters of adolescence\n进入青春期 ↔ 进入波涛汹涌的青春期\n【辨邻】 C C 段说 teenage crime, drug abuse and premarital sex are in general decline，落\n点是 趋势安抚，与题干 父母担心 正好相反。\nteenage crime, drug abuse and premarital sex are in general decline", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Even during the turbulent years of last century, youth rebellion was often exaggerated in the media.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】上世纪青年叛逆被夸大\n【定位】Yet even then reports of widespread youth rebellion were overdone: Most kids in the '60s and\n'70s shared their parents' basic values.\n可即便在那时候，关于青年大规模叛逆的报道也是被过度渲染的：60、70 年代的大多数孩\n子其实认同父母的基本价值观。\n【改写】Even during the turbulent years of last century ↔ Most kids in the '60s and '70s\n上世纪动荡岁月 ↔ 60、70 年代\nyouth rebellion ↔ widespread youth rebellion\n原样复现\noften exaggerated in the media ↔ reports of widespread youth rebellion were overdone\nexaggerated ↔ overdone + reports ↔ in the media\n【辨邻】 B B 段聚焦 In October 2000 当下的媒体呈现，与题干所问的 上世纪叛逆是否被夸大 时\n段错位。\nin a recent month of TV news coverage of American youth", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Teenagers of today often turn to their parents for advice on such important matters as career choice.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】在择业等大事上找父母\n【定位】Today's teenagers admire their parents and welcome parental guidance about important\nmatters such as career choice—though certainly not Mom and Dad's advice on matters of\npersonal taste, such as music or fashion.\n今天的青少年钦佩父母，并欢迎父母在诸如择业之类重要事情上的指导——当然不是音乐、\n时尚等个人品味方面的意见。\n【改写】turn to their parents for advice ↔ welcome parental guidance\n向父母求教 ↔ 欢迎父母指导\non such important matters as career choice ↔ important matters such as career choice\nsuch ... as 与 such as 的近乎逐字重合", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "The incidence of teenage crime and misbehavior is decreasing nowadays.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】青少年犯罪与不良行为下降\n【定位】From other sources, we also know teenage crime, drug abuse and premarital sex are in\ngeneral decline.\n从其他来源我们也知道，青少年犯罪、吸毒和婚前性行为整体上在下降。\n【改写】teenage crime and misbehavior ↔ teenage crime, drug abuse and premarital sex\nmisbehavior 概括 drug abuse 与 premarital sex\nis decreasing nowadays ↔ are in general decline\n在下降 的两种表达\n【辨邻】 B B 段说 the criminal justice system accounted for nearly one out of every five\nvisual backgrounds，是统计电视新闻画面，与 青少年实际犯罪率下降 不是同一\n回事。\nthe criminal justice system accounted for nearly one out of every five visual backgrounds", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Young people should have lofty ideals in life and strive to be leaders.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】崇高理想 + 立志为领袖\n【定位】But there is also a place in a young life for noble purposes that include a dedication\nto the broader society, a love of country and an aspiration to make their own\nleadership contributions.\n但年轻人的生活里也应该有崇高的目标——包括对更广阔社会的奉献、对国家的热爱，以及\n做出自身领导贡献的志向。\n【改写】lofty ideals in life ↔ noble purposes\nlofty ↔ noble 同义词替换\nstrive to be leaders ↔ an aspiration to make their own leadership contributions\n动词短语 ↔ 名词化短语\n【辨邻】 L L 段强调 Our society needs the full participation of its younger citizens，落脚是\n参与社会、塑造未来；题干 strive to be leaders 的直接出处仍在 K 段。\nOur society needs the full participation of its younger citizens if it is to continue to thrive", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Some young people like to keep something to themselves and don't want their parents to know about it.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】私藏不让父母知道的东西\n【定位】Although more than a third have an object in their rooms they would like to keep secret from\ntheir parents, rarely is it anything more alarming than a diary or off-color(低俗的) book\nor CD.\n尽管超过三分之一的青少年房间里有件不愿让父母知道的东西，但很少比一本日记、一本低\n俗的书或 CD 更可怕。\n【改写】Some young people like to keep something to themselves ↔\nmore than a third have an object in their rooms they would like to keep secret\n持有私物 = 留东西给自己\ndon't want their parents to know about it ↔ keep secret from their parents\n对父母保密 ↔ 不想让父母知道", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "It is beneficial to encourage young people to explore the broader world and get ready to make it a better place.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】鼓励青年探索世界 + 让世界更好\n【定位】We have everything to gain by encouraging them to explore the world beyond their\nimmediate experience and to prepare themselves for their turn at shaping that world.\n鼓励他们走出眼下经验、去探索更广阔的世界，并为自己塑造世界的那一天做好准备——我\n们将受益匪浅。\n【改写】It is beneficial ↔ We have everything to gain by\n受益匪浅 ↔ 有益\nencourage young people to explore the broader world ↔\nencouraging them to explore the world beyond their immediate experience\n广阔的世界 ↔ 眼前经验之外的世界\nget ready to make it a better place ↔ prepare themselves for their turn at shaping that world\n为更好地塑造 ↔ 让它更好\n【辨邻】 K K 段说 there is also a place in a young life for noble purposes，是 该有 的立论；\n题干 It is beneficial to encourage ... 是 鼓励有益 的呼吁，与 L 段对位。\nthere is also a place in a young life for noble purposes", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Many teenagers now offer to render service to the needy.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】为弱势 / 有需要的人服务\n【定位】Many of them volunteer for community service with disadvantaged people.\n他们中很多人自愿参与服务弱势群体的社区志愿活动。\n【改写】offer to render service ↔ volunteer for community service\n主动提供服务 ↔ 自愿做社区服务\nthe needy ↔ disadvantaged people\nthe + 形容词 = 一类人", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Interviews with students find many of them are only concerned about personal matters.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】访谈中学生只关心个人事务\n【定位】Asked what they would like to change in the world, the students mentioned only personal\nconcerns such as slowing down the pace of life, gaining good friends, becoming more\nspiritual, becoming either more materially successful or less materially oriented (depending\non the student's values), and being more respectful of the Earth, animals and other people.\n被问到想改变世界中的什么时，学生们只提到了个人层面的事，比如放慢生活节奏、交到好\n朋友、变得更有精神追求、变得更物质成功或更不物质化（视个人价值观而定），以及更尊\n重地球、动物和他人。\n【改写】Interviews with students ↔ In our interviews\n介词短语 ↔ 名词化主语\nmany of them are only concerned about personal matters ↔\nthe students mentioned only personal concerns\nconcerned about ↔ personal concerns 名词化变体\n【辨邻】 H H 段说 in follow-up interviews with a few of the teenagers, we found ... little\ninterest in civic life，落脚是 缺乏公共兴趣，没有 学生只提个人事务 的集中呈现，故\n不是源头段。\nin follow-up interviews with a few of the teenagers, we found lots of insight, positive feeling and\ninspirational thinking\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 总括开篇：USA WEEKEND 的青少年与父母调查显示，今天的青少年与父母关系融\n洽、对家庭教养方式认可；提到很多孩子房间里藏有不愿让父母知道的东西，但多半\n只是日记或低俗的书 / CD。\n信息指纹 日记 / 低俗书或 CD 等私藏物\nB\n媒体对青少年的呈现严重失衡：电视新闻里几乎不出现青少年在家、工作的画面，反\n倒近五分之一画面与刑事司法相关；正因如此，父母才会担心自家孩子进入青春期会\n失控。\n信息指纹 媒体画面与父母担心孩子失控\nC 用事实安抚读者：今天的青少年比刻板印象中的形象更善良、理智、快乐；其他来源\n也显示青少年犯罪、吸毒和婚前性行为整体在下降。\n信息指纹 青少年犯罪 / 吸毒 / 婚前性行为整体下降\nD\n作者用自己在 Stanford 青少年研究中心的访谈研究印证 USA WEEKEND 的结论：青少\n年钦佩父母、欢迎父母在重要事项（如择业）上的指导，但不接受父母对个人品味\n（音乐、时尚）的意见；选英雄时多选家中长辈。\n信息指纹 在择业等重要事项上欢迎父母指导\nE 多数青少年认为应宽容个体差异，许多人为弱势群体做志愿服务；他们关于自己、朋\n友和家人的描述总体呈现强烈正面情绪。\n信息指纹 为弱势群体做志愿服务\nF 回顾代际关系：一代人之前的 代沟 概念被夸大，60–70 年代的青少年其实大体认同父\n母价值观；如今家庭关系更紧密，可能因社会平稳、不良行为危害已知、全球化背景\n下家更像避风港，以及父母重新承担起指导角色。\n信息指纹 上世纪青年叛逆被夸大\nG 笔锋一转：这些数据里看不到今天的年轻人对国家、对更广阔的公民政治环境或社会\n未来的关心；他们倾向于内向化，对身边的亲密关系有益，却往往牺牲了与外部世界\n的联系。\n信息指纹 青年向内转、忽视公共与未来社会\nH John Templeton 基金会的 生命法则 征文项目中，作者团队分析了 400 多篇青少年文\n章及随后的访谈，发现孩子们有洞见和正能量，但对家人和密友之外的公民生活兴趣\n寥寥。\n信息指纹 生命法则 征文与访谈\nI\n举例说明青年远离公共领域：只有一个男孩想当总统；当前 18–24 岁年轻选民投票率\n严重下滑，国家选举中仅四分之一多一点去投票，约为 18 岁刚获选举权时的一半。\n信息指纹 18–24 岁年轻选民投票率骤降\nJ\n访谈中许多学生对政治怀疑反感，称政客不诚实、个人无力改变；被问到想改变世界\n的什么时，他们只提个人层面的事（放慢生活节奏、交好友、追求精神 / 物质等），\n一个男孩甚至说宁愿专心搞艺术也不想 拯救世界。\n信息指纹 访谈中学生只关心个人议题\nK\n作者立论：培养个人兴趣和家庭友谊固然好，但年轻人的生活里也应有 崇高目标——\n奉献社会、热爱祖国、立志承担领导贡献。\n信息指纹 年轻人应有崇高目标、立志当领袖\nL\n结尾呼吁：过去青年积极参与国家服务和公民事务，今天若不再如此就要问为什么；\n社会需要年轻公民充分参与，鼓励他们走出自身经验、为塑造未来世界做好准备，我\n们将受益匪浅。\n信息指纹 鼓励青年走出自身、塑造未来", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do the revised \"Green Guides\" require businesses to do?", "options": ["A) Manufacture as many green products as possible.", "B) Indicate whether their products are recyclable.", "C) Specify in what way their products are green.", "D) Attach green labels to all of their products."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do the revised \"Green Guides\" require businesses to do? 直接问规则要求做什\n么，答案应是原文规定句的客观转述。\n【拆句】主干 What do the revised \"Green Guides\" require businesses to do，疑问代词 What\n作 do 的宾语；revised 作前置定语修饰 Green Guides，businesses 是 require 的宾语，\nto do 为不定式作宾语补足语。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Marketers must qualify their claims on the product packaging and limit them to a specific\nbenefit, such as how much of the product is recycled.\n营销者必须在产品包装上对其宣称加以限定，并将其约束在某一具体益处上，例如产品中有\n多少比例是循环再造的。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nManufacture as many green products as possible 把对\"宣传方式\"的规定偷换为\n对\"生产数量\"的要求，原文从未提及。\nB) 以偏概全\n原文 such as how much of the product is recycled 用 such as 把\"可回收\"作举例，\nIndicate whether their products are recyclable 却把这一例子当作唯一要求。\nC) ✓ 正确\nlimit them to a specific benefit, such as how much of the product is recycled ↔\nSpecify in what way their products are green\nqualify their claims ↔ Specify\nC 项把\"加以限定\"和\"指明具体益处\"两层语义合并为一句\"specify in what way their\nproducts are green\"，与预判完全吻合。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 warn marketers against using labels that make broad claims 是\"警告别乱贴宽\n泛标签\"，Attach green labels to all of their products 反过来要求\"全部贴绿标\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do the revised \"Green Guides\" require businesses to do?", "options": ["A) Manufacture as many green products as possible.", "B) Indicate whether their products are recyclable.", "C) Specify in what way their products are green.", "D) Attach green labels to all of their products."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do the revised \"Green Guides\" require businesses to do? 仍是直问规则要求的细\n节题。\n【拆句】结构与 46 题完全相同：What 作 do 的宾语，revised 修饰 Green Guides，businesses 作\nrequire 的宾语，to do 作宾补；无理解陷阱。\n【定位】Marketers must qualify their claims on the product packaging and limit them to a specific\nbenefit, such as how much of the product is recycled.\n营销者必须在产品包装上对其宣称加以限定，并将其约束在某一具体益处上，例如产品中有\n多少比例是循环再造的。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nManufacture as many green products as possible 与原文\"宣传方式\"无关。\nB) 以偏概全\nIndicate whether their products are recyclable 把原文 such as how much of the\nproduct is recycled 这一例子当作唯一要求。\nC) ✓ 正确\nlimit them to a specific benefit ↔ Specify in what way their products are green\nC 项把\"加以限定\"与\"指明具体益处\"合并为一句，与预判完全一致。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 warn marketers against using labels that make broad claims 是\"警告别乱\n贴\"，Attach green labels to all of their products 反过来要求\"全贴\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What was SC Johnson accused of in the class-action lawsuits?", "options": ["A) It gave consumers the impression that all its products were truly green.", "B) It gave a third party the authority to label its products as environmentally friendly.", "C) It misled consumers to believe that its products had been certified by a third party.", "D) It sold cleaning products that were not included in the official \"Greenlist\"."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What was SC Johnson accused of in the class-action lawsuits? 直接问被指控的具体内\n容，对应原文叙述句。\n【拆句】主干 What was SC Johnson accused of，被动语态：SC Johnson 是受动者；in the\nclass-action lawsuits 作时间/事件状语，限定指控发生的场合。注意 accused of 后接的\n是\"指控的内容\"，原文对应表达可能是 misleading/gave the impression that…，需识别\n这一对应。\n【定位】The lawsuits said that the label was misleading because it gave the impression that the\nproducts had been certified by a third party when the certification was the company's own.\n诉讼指控该标签具有误导性，因为它给人留下产品已经过第三方认证的印象，而实际上该认\n证是公司自身做出的。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt gave consumers the impression that all its products were truly green 把指控重\n点从\"经第三方认证\"偷换为\"产品全都真正环保\"，且 all 与 truly 在原文均无对应。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt gave a third party the authority to label its products as environmentally\nfriendly 说\"授权第三方贴标\"，正是原文否认的——原文为 the certification was the\ncompany's own，反向陈述。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe label was misleading ↔ It misled consumers\ngave the impression that the products had been certified by a third party ↔\nto believe that its products had been certified by a third party\nC 项把\"标签误导\"与\"让人以为经第三方认证\"两层语义合并，并与定位句一一对应，与预\n判吻合。\nD) 无中生有\nIt sold cleaning products that were not included in the official \"Greenlist\" 编造\"卖\n了不在官方 Greenlist 里的清洁产品\"，原文从未提及。原文 Greenlist 也并非\"官方\"清\n单，而是公司自有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "How did Christopher Beard defend his company's labeling practice?", "options": ["A) There were no clear guidelines concerning green labeling.", "B) His company's products had been well received by the public.", "C) It was in conformity to the prevailing practice in the market.", "D) No law required the involvement of a third party in certification."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How did Christopher Beard defend his company's labeling practice? 问的是\"用何种说\n法辩护\"，答案在原话里。\n【拆句】How 作方式状语，主干 did Christopher Beard defend his company's labeling\npractice。注意 his company's labeling practice 是\"公司的标签做法\"——即 Greenlist 的\n标注行为；考生若误读为\"产品\"会偏。\n【定位】\"We are very proud of our accomplishments under the Greenlist system and we believe that\nwe will prevail in these cases,\" Christopher Beard, director of public affairs for SC Johnson,\nsaid, while acknowledging that \"this has been an area that is difficult to navigate.\"\n\"我们对 Greenlist 体系下取得的成就深感自豪，并且相信会在这些案件中胜诉。\"SC\nJohnson 公共事务总监 Christopher Beard 这样说，同时也承认\"这一直是一个难以把握的\n领域\"。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthis has been an area that is difficult to navigate ↔\nThere were no clear guidelines concerning green labeling\nA 项把\"难以把握\"具象化为\"没有明确指引\"，正是 Beard 用以为公司开脱的核心辩\n护点。\nB) 答非所问\nHis company's products had been well received by the public 谈\"产品受欢迎\"，原\n文未提及；Beard 提的是\"自豪于 Greenlist 体系下的成就\"，并非\"公众反响好\"。\nC) 无中生有\nIt was in conformity to the prevailing practice in the market 说\"符合行业普遍做\n法\"，原文中 Beard 从未做过\"大家都这么干\"的辩解。\nD) 无中生有\nNo law required the involvement of a third party in certification 称\"法律没要求第\n三方介入\"，原文未提任何法律层面的辩护。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does Kevin Wilhelm imply by saying \"It's kind of a Wild West\" (Line 3, Para. 11)?", "options": ["A) Businesses compete to produce green products.", "B) Each business acts its own way in green labeling.", "C) Consumers grow wild with products labeled green.", "D) Anything produced in the West can be labeled green."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （词义句意题）\n【判型】What does Kevin Wilhelm imply by saying \"It's kind of a Wild West\" 问比喻句的言下\n之意，属句意题。\n【拆句】What 作 imply 的宾语，by saying \"…\" 作方式状语；引语 It's kind of a Wild West 是名\n词性比喻——Wild West（蛮荒西部）象征\"无序、无规、各自为政\"。考生需识别比喻义而\n非字面义。\n【定位】\"It's kind of a Wild West, anybody can claim themselves to be green.\"\n\"这有点像野西部，谁都可以宣称自己是环保的。\"\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nBusinesses compete to produce green products 谈的是\"竞相生产\"，原文谈的是\"自\n称环保\"——重点是宣传/贴标，不是生产。\nB) ✓ 正确\nanybody can claim themselves to be green ↔\nEach business acts its own way in green labeling\nWild West ↔ Each business acts its own way\nB 项精准抓住\"野西部=无统一规则、各自为政\"的核心意涵，且与引语后半句的同位解释\n完全吻合。\nC) 偷换概念\nConsumers grow wild with products labeled green 把\"野西部\"的 wild 字面附着到\nconsumers 头上，谈\"消费者疯狂\"，与原文谈\"行业无序\"完全两码事。\nD) 偷换概念\nAnything produced in the West can be labeled green 把 Wild West 误解为字面地\n理意义上的\"西部\"，并嫁接到\"产地→可贴绿标\"的奇怪结论上。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "词义句意题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn about America's education system?", "options": ["A) It provides a ladder of opportunity for the wealthy.", "B) It contributes little to the elimination of inequality.", "C) It has remained basically unchanged for generations.", "D) It has brought up generations of responsible citizens."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about America's education system? 是问\"关于美国教育体系我们了解\n到什么\"，要的是原文已说出的事实表述，而不是隐含推断。\n【拆句】What do we learn about America's education system? 是标准疑问句，主干 we learn\nabout America's education system，无从句、无否定、无比较，理解上无陷阱。\n【定位】America's education system has become less a ladder of opportunity than a structure to\ntransmit inequality from one generation to the next.\n美国的教育体系已不再那么像一架通向机会的阶梯，而更像一座把不平等代代相传的结构。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文说教育体系不再是\"机会的阶梯\"，选项 It provides a ladder of opportunity for\nthe wealthy. 反过来说它\"为富人提供机会的阶梯\"，无中生有地嫁接了\"the wealthy\"这\n一对象。\nB) ✓ 正确\na structure to transmit inequality from one generation to the next ↔\ncontributes little to the elimination of inequality\nless a ladder of opportunity than a structure to transmit inequality ↔ contributes little\n预判要的是\"几乎没在消除不平等 / 反而在传递不平等\"两层意思；选项 It contributes\nlittle to the elimination of inequality. 正好覆盖前一层，是定位句的同义反向改写，因\n此锁定 B。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 has become less ... than ... 强调\"已经发生了变化\"，选项 It has remained\nbasically unchanged for generations. 说\"几代未变\"，与原文方向完全相反。\nD) 无中生有\n原文通篇批判教育体系，选项 It has brought up generations of responsible citizens.\n给出正向评价（培养了一代代负责任的公民），属作者立场之反，且原文无任何\"培养公\n民\"的提及。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is chiefly responsible for the undesirable performance of inner-city schools?", "options": ["A) Unqualified teachers.", "B) Lack of financial resources.", "C) Unfavorable learning environment.", "D) Subconscious racial discrimination."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is chiefly responsible for the undesirable performance of inner-city schools? 是\n问明确给出的主因事实，原文必然有一句直接的因果断言。\n【拆句】题干主干 What is chiefly responsible for X，be responsible for 意为\"对……负责\"，引申\n为\"造成……的原因\"；undesirable performance 即\"差劲的表现\"。\n【定位】It's true that the main reason inner-city schools do poorly isn't teachers' unions, but poverty.\n确实，市中心学校表现糟糕的主要原因不是教师工会，而是贫困。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文把工会和教师列为\"不是主因\"，选项 Unqualified teachers. 把\"工会\"偷换为\"不合\n格教师\"——确实下文有差教师的话题，但作为\"主因\"原文明确否定。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe main reason inner-city schools do poorly isn't teachers' unions, but poverty ↔\nLack of financial resources.\n预判核心\"贫困是主因\"对应到选项里只有 Lack of financial resources. 是同义复述，其\n余选项均未提到资金/贫困。\nC) 无中生有\n选项 Unfavorable learning environment. 谈\"不利的学习环境\"，原文从头到尾未将\"环\n境\"列为差表现的主因。\nD) 无中生有\n选项 Subconscious racial discrimination. 引入了\"潜意识种族歧视\"这一话题，全文没\n有任何提及种族的内容。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author think the union should do to win popular support?", "options": ["A) Assist the city government in reforming schools.", "B) Give constructive advice to inner-city schools.", "C) Demand higher pay for teachers.", "D) Help teachers improve teaching."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think the union should do to win popular support? 问作者明确\n表达的建议，原文有让步条件句直接对应。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author think (that) the union should do to win popular\nsupport；嵌套宾语从句 + 不定式目的状语 to win popular support（\"以赢得民众\n支持\"）。\n【定位】I'd be sympathetic if the union focused solely on higher compensation. Teachers need\nto be much better paid to attract the best college graduates to the nation's worst schools.\nBut, instead, the Chicago union seems to be using its political capital primarily to protect\nweak performers.\n如果工会只聚焦于争取更高薪酬，我是同情（支持）的。教师确需大幅加薪，才能把最优秀\n的大学毕业生吸引到全国最差的学校。可惜，芝加哥工会似乎正把它的政治资本主要用在保\n护差表现者上。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 the union is resisting 明确写工会在抵制市长的改革，选项 Assist the city\ngovernment in reforming schools. 说工会\"协助政府改革\"，与原文反向。\nB) 无中生有\n选项 Give constructive advice to inner-city schools. 让工会\"向市中心学校建言\"，原\n文从未让工会扮演建议者，且作者认为工会该做的是争取薪酬，不是建言。\nC) ✓ 正确\nfocused solely on higher compensation ↔ Demand higher pay for teachers.\nTeachers need to be much better paid ↔ higher pay for teachers\n预判核心\"工会应聚焦于更高薪酬\"几乎逐字对应 Demand higher pay for teachers.，\n锁定 C。\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 Help teachers improve teaching. 让工会\"帮助教师提升教学\"，原文从未谈及工\n会负责教学培训；且原文反复点出工会的问题在\"保护差表现者\"而非\"提升教学\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is the finding of the gold standard study by Harvard and Columbia University scholars?", "options": ["A) Many inner-city school teachers are not equal to their jobs.", "B) A large proportion of inner-city children often miss classes.", "C) Many students are dissatisfied with their teachers.", "D) Student performance has a lot to do with teachers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the finding of the gold standard study 问的是该研究明面写出的结论，属事实\n复述。\n【拆句】题干主干 What is the finding of the study；介词短语 by Harvard and Columbia\nUniversity scholars 修饰 study，说明研究执行者，无理解陷阱。\n【定位】The gold standard study by Harvard and Columbia University scholars found that even in\nhigh-poverty schools, teachers consistently had a huge positive or negative impact.\n这项由哈佛与哥伦比亚大学学者完成的黄金标准研究发现：即便在高贫困学校里，教师始终\n对学生有巨大的正面或负面影响。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n原文只说\"教师之间差异巨大、有强有弱\"，选项 Many inner-city school teachers are\nnot equal to their jobs. 偷换为\"很多教师不胜任\"——把双向差异（正面 or 负面）单\n方面强化为\"普遍不胜任\"。\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 A large proportion of inner-city children often miss classes. 说\"很大比例的市\n中心学生经常缺课\"，原文 as if a child misses 40% of the school year 是\"差老师对学\n生的影响相当于缺课 40%\"的比喻，并非真有学生缺课。\nC) 无中生有\n选项 Many students are dissatisfied with their teachers. 谈\"学生对老师不满\"，原文\n研究讨论的是教师对学生的客观影响，从未涉及学生主观满意度。\nD) ✓ 正确\nteachers consistently had a huge positive or negative impact ↔\nStudent performance has a lot to do with teachers.\nstrong teachers in the fourth through eighth grades raised the skills of their students in ways that\nwould last for decades\n↔ Student performance has a lot to do with teachers.\n预判核心\"教师差异对学生有巨大影响\"对应 Student performance has a lot to do\nwith teachers.——这是因果方向相同、主宾互换的同义改写，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "Why does the author say the Chicago union's demand is an insult to students?", "options": ["A) It protects incompetent teachers at the expense of students.", "B) It underestimates students' ability to tell good teachers from poor ones.", "C) It makes students feel that they are discriminated against in many ways.", "D) It totally ignores students' initiative in the learning process."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why does the author say the Chicago union's demand is an insult to students? 问的\n是作者已说出\"insult\"的直接理由，原文必有同段相邻的因果支撑。\n【拆句】题干主干 Why does the author say [the Chicago union's demand is an insult to\nstudents]；say 的宾语是一个完整从句，意思是\"芝加哥工会的诉求是对学生的侮辱\"。\n【定位】Unfortunately, the union in Chicago is insisting that teachers who are laid off—often for\nbeing ineffective—should get priority in new hiring. That's an insult to students.\n可惜，芝加哥工会坚持要求：被解雇的教师（这些教师常常是因为表现差才被解雇）在新一\n轮招聘中应享有优先权。这是对学生的侮辱。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nteachers who are laid off—often for being ineffective—should get priority in new hiring ↔\nIt protects incompetent teachers at the expense of students.\nto protect weak performers ↔ It protects incompetent teachers\n预判要的两层意思——\"保护差教师\"+ \"牺牲学生\" ——只有 It protects incompetent\nteachers at the expense of students. 完整命中，锁定 A。\nB) 无中生有\n选项 It underestimates students' ability to tell good teachers from poor ones.\n谈\"低估学生分辨好坏老师的能力\"，原文从未谈及学生的分辨力。\nC) 无中生有\n选项 It makes students feel that they are discriminated against in many ways. 引\n入\"歧视\"话题，全文未提任何歧视议题。\nD) 无中生有\n选项 It totally ignores students' initiative in the learning process. 谈\"完全忽视学生\n在学习过程中的主动性\"，原文未涉及学生主动性话题。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
